                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Personality disorders are a group of mental health conditions characterized by long-term patterns of behavior that deviate from cultural norms and cause significant distress or impairment in social, occupational, or other areas of functioning. Symptoms typically emerge in adolescence or early adulthood and persist throughout life. There are ten recognized personality disorders grouped into three clusters: Cluster A (odd or eccentric), Cluster B (dramatic, emotional, or erratic), and Cluster C (anxious or fearful).\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the symptoms of schizotypal personality disorder?\n2. What are the symptoms of generalized anxiety disorder?\n3. What are the symptoms of avoidant personality disorder?\n4. What are the symptoms of paranoid personality disorder?\n5. What are the symptoms of dependent personality disorder?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Symptoms of schizotypal personality disorder include odd beliefs or magical thinking, unusual perceptual experiences, odd thinking and speech, suspiciousness or paranoid ideation, inappropriate or constricted affect, lack of close friends, and excessive social anxiety.\n2. Symptoms of generalized anxiety disorder include excessive anxiety and worry about a variety of events or activities, difficulty controlling worry, restlessness or feeling on edge, fatigue, difficulty concentrating, irritability, muscle tension, and sleep disturbance.\n3. Symptoms of avoidant personality disorder include a pervasive pattern of social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy, and hypersensitivity to negative evaluation, as well as avoiding social or occupational activities that involve significant interpersonal contact due to fear of criticism, disapproval, or rejection.\n4. Symptoms of paranoid personality disorder include a pervasive distrust and suspiciousness of others, interpreting others' motives as malevolent, reluctance to confide in others, bearing grudges, and perceiving attacks on their character or reputation that are not apparent to others.\n5. Symptoms of dependent personality disorder include a pervasive and excessive need to be taken care of, submissive and clinging behavior, fear of separation, difficulty making decisions without reassurance or advice from others, feeling helpless or uncomfortable when alone, and preoccupation with fears of being left to care for oneself.\n\nAnswer: C) Avoidant personality disorder is the most likely explanation for the daughter's behavior based on her symptoms of social isolation, fear of criticism, and low self-esteem. The daughter's reluctance to participate in extracurricular activities or invite friends over, as well as her belief that she is untalented and unattractive, are consistent with the avoidant personality disorder criteria of social inhibition, feelings of inadequacy, and hypersensitivity",
