                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings.\n2. Consider the patient's medical history and medication use.\n3. Evaluate the possible causes of the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings.\n4. Choose the most likely cause based on the information gathered.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms of painful double vision that occurs only when looking sideways, along with the physical exam findings of an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye when looking in the opposite direction, the most likely cause is demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus (MLF). The MLF is responsible for coordinating eye movements, and demyelination can disrupt this coordination, leading to the patient's symptoms and physical exam findings.\n\nThe patient's medical history of a recent fall and normal CT scan make a compressive tumor or subdural hematoma less likely. Thiamine deficiency is not a common cause of these symptoms and would not explain the disc hyperemia seen on fundoscopy. Infarction of the brainstem could cause similar symptoms, but the patient's young age and lack of risk factors make this less likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus.",
