                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The patient has myopia and has been wearing corrective lenses for 10 years. She also recently fell off her bike and had a CT scan of the head that showed no abnormalities. She is currently experiencing periodic painful double vision when looking sideways, with an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the medial longitudinal fasciculus?\n2. What are the symptoms of demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus?\n3. What is a compressive tumor of the pons?\n4. What are the symptoms of a compressive tumor of the pons?\n5. What is thiamine deficiency?\n6. What are the symptoms of oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency?\n7. What is a subdural hematoma?\n8. What are the symptoms of a subdural hematoma?\n9. What is brainstem infarction?\n10. What are the symptoms of brainstem infarction?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The medial longitudinal fasciculus is a bundle of nerve fibers that connects the cranial nerve nuclei responsible for eye movement.\n2. Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus can cause internuclear ophthalmoplegia, which is characterized by an adduction deficit in one eye and nystagmus in the other eye.\n3. A compressive tumor of the pons is a tumor that grows in the pons, which is a part of the brainstem that controls many important functions such as breathing, sleeping, and bladder control.\n4. Symptoms of a compressive tumor of the pons can include double vision, difficulty speaking or swallowing, weakness or numbness in the face or limbs, and changes in breathing or heart rate.\n5. Thiamine deficiency is a condition that occurs when the body doesn't have enough thiamine (vitamin B1).\n6. Symptoms of oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency can include confusion, memory loss, and eye movement abnormalities.\n7. A subdural hematoma is a type of bleeding that occurs between the brain and the outermost layer of the brain (the dura).\n8. Symptoms of a subdural hematoma can include headache, confusion, dizziness, and changes in vision.\n9. Brainstem infarction is a type of stroke that occurs when blood flow to the brainstem is blocked.\n10. Symptoms of brainstem infarction can include double vision, difficulty speaking or swallowing, weakness or numb",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "9f6c3c4b-59cf-40cf-928b-de58e775ed71",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-E",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus, as the patient's symptoms match those of internuclear ophthalmoplegia, which is a common symptom of demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus. The patient's CT scan showed no abnormalities, making options B, D, and E less likely. Thiamine deficiency (option C) can cause eye movement abnormalities, but the patient's symptoms are more consistent with internuclear ophthalmoplegia.",
