                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what adduction deficit means?\n\nProfessor: Adduction is the movement of the eye towards the nose. An adduction deficit means that the eye is unable to move towards the nose.\n\nStudent: What is nystagmus?\n\nProfessor: Nystagmus is an involuntary movement of the eye, which can be horizontal, vertical or rotary.\n\nStudent: What does bilateral disc hyperemia mean?\n\nProfessor: Bilateral disc hyperemia means that both optic discs appear red and swollen.\n\nStudent: What is the medial longitudinal fasciculus?\n\nProfessor: The medial longitudinal fasciculus is a bundle of nerve fibers that connects the cranial nerve nuclei responsible for eye movement.\n\nStudent: What is a compressive tumor of the pons?\n\nProfessor: The pons is a part of the brainstem that controls many vital functions. A compressive tumor of the pons means that there is a growth in that area that is putting pressure on the surrounding structures.\n\nStudent: What is oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency?\n\nProfessor: Thiamine is a vitamin that is essential for the proper functioning of the nervous system. Oxidative damage due to thiamine deficiency means that there is damage to the nervous system caused by a lack of thiamine.\n\nStudent: What is a subdural hematoma?\n\nProfessor: A subdural hematoma is a collection of blood between the brain and the outermost layer of the brain called the dura mater.\n\nStudent: What is brainstem infarction?\n\nProfessor: Brainstem infarction is a type of stroke that occurs when the blood supply to the brainstem is interrupted, leading to damage of the brain tissue. \n\nProfessor: Based on the patient's symptoms and findings, the most likely cause of her condition is A) Demyelination of the medial longitudinal fasciculus.",
                        "answers": [
