                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient's symptoms and physical examination findings are consistent with giant cell arteritis (GCA), which is confirmed by the biopsy results. Treatment with high-dose corticosteroids is appropriate for GCA. However, GCA is associated with several potential complications, including:\n\nA) Pulmonary hypertension: This is not a common complication of GCA and is not directly related to the patient's symptoms or physical examination findings.\n\nB) Lower extremity gangrene: This is not a common complication of GCA and is not directly related to the patient's symptoms or physical examination findings.\n\nC) Palpable purpura: This is not a common complication of GCA and is not directly related to the patient's symptoms or physical examination findings.\n\nD) Posterior uveitis: This is a potential complication of GCA, as the inflammation can affect the blood vessels supplying the retina. However, the patient's symptoms and physical examination findings suggest involvement of the anterior portion of the eye (double vision and inward deviation of the left eye), rather than the posterior portion.\n\nE) Thoracic aortic aneurysm: This is a potential complication of GCA, as the inflammation can affect the aorta. However, this complication typically occurs in patients with longstanding, untreated GCA, rather than in patients who are receiving appropriate treatment with corticosteroids.\n\nTherefore, the most likely complication for this patient is D) Posterior uveitis, although it is important to monitor for other potential complications as well.",
