                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe patient is experiencing sudden onset speech changes, which suggests a problem with language processing. This symptom is known as aphasia. \n\nThe patient also has weakness in the right arm, which suggests a problem with motor function. \n\nBased on these symptoms, we can narrow down the possible locations of damage to the brain. \n\nOption A: Left frontal lobe - Damage to the left frontal lobe can cause expressive aphasia, which affects the ability to produce language. However, weakness in the right arm is not typically associated with damage to this area. \n\nOption B: Left internal capsule - Damage to the left internal capsule can cause both expressive and receptive aphasia, as well as weakness on the opposite side of the body (in this case, the right arm). This is a possible location of damage for this patient. \n\nOption C: Left temporal lobe - Damage to the left temporal lobe can cause receptive aphasia, which affects the ability to understand language. This does not explain the patient's weakness in the right arm. \n\nOption D: Right frontal lobe - Damage to the right frontal lobe can cause weakness on the opposite side of the body (in this case, the left arm), but it does not typically cause aphasia. \n\nOption E: Right temporal lobe - Damage to the right temporal lobe can cause difficulty with visual and auditory processing, but it does not typically cause aphasia or weakness in the opposite side of the body. \n\nTherefore, the best answer is B) Left internal capsule.",
