                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, can you explain the question again?\n\nProfessor: Sure, a 78-year-old man suddenly had speech changes and weakness in his right arm. We need to determine which location in the brain is most likely damaged to explain these symptoms.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what are the options?\n\nProfessor: The options are A) Left frontal lobe, B) Left internal capsule, C) Left temporal lobe, D) Right frontal lobe, and E) Right temporal lobe.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what each of these locations does?\n\nProfessor: The left frontal lobe is responsible for language production and speech. The left internal capsule contains the motor fibers that control movement of the right arm. The left temporal lobe is involved in language comprehension. The right frontal lobe is responsible for movement and the right temporal lobe is involved in visual and auditory processing.\n\nStudent: Okay, so which location do you think is most likely damaged?\n\nProfessor: Based on the symptoms of distorted speech and weakness in the right arm, the most likely location to be damaged is the left internal capsule.\n\nStudent: Can you explain why?\n\nProfessor: The left internal capsule contains the motor fibers that control movement of the right arm. Damage to this area can cause weakness or paralysis of the right arm. Additionally, the patient's speech changes suggest that the left frontal lobe, which is responsible for language production, may also be affected. However, the weakness in the right arm is a more specific symptom that points to the left internal capsule as the most likely location of damage.",
