                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to note that ovarian cancer typically spreads through the lymphatic system rather than the bloodstream. The lymphatic system is a network of vessels and organs that helps to remove waste and fight infections. Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that filter lymph fluid and contain immune cells that can help to fight cancer cells.\n\nIn this case, the most likely lymphatic route for ovarian cancer to have metastasized to the brain would be option C: Sentinel node - right lumbar trunk - cisterna chyli - right bronchomediastinal trunk - right lymphatic duct - right subclavian vein - systemic circulation.\n\nThe sentinel node is the first lymph node that cancer cells are likely to spread to from the primary tumor. From there, the cancer cells can travel through the lymphatic vessels to the lumbar trunk, which is a large lymphatic vessel that drains lymph fluid from the lower body. The cisterna chyli is a sac-like structure at the base of the thoracic duct where lymph fluid from the lower body and intestines collects before entering the bloodstream.\n\nThe right bronchomediastinal trunk is a lymphatic vessel that drains lymph fluid from the right side of the chest and upper body. From there, the cancer cells can enter the right lymphatic duct, which is a large lymphatic vessel that drains lymph fluid from the right side of the head, neck, and upper body. The right lymphatic duct then empties into the right subclavian vein, which is a large vein that carries blood from the arm and shoulder to the heart.\n\nOnce the cancer cells enter the bloodstream, they can travel to distant organs such as the lungs and brain, where they can form new tumors and continue to grow. It is important to note that this is just one possible lymphatic route for ovarian cancer to metastasize to the brain, and other routes may also be possible depending on the location and extent of the primary tumor.",
