                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "1. Sentinel node: This is the first lymph node that cancer cells are most likely to spread to from the primary tumor. In the case of ovarian cancer, the sentinel nodes are usually located in the pelvic region.\n\n2. Lumbar trunks: The lymph from the pelvic region drains into the lumbar trunks. Since the patient is an Asian woman, it is more likely that the cancer cells would have spread to the left lumbar trunk.\n\n3. Cisterna chyli: The left lumbar trunk drains into the cisterna chyli, which is a large sac-like structure that collects lymph from the lower half of the body.\n\n4. Thoracic duct: The cisterna chyli continues as the thoracic duct, which is the largest lymphatic vessel in the body. It carries lymph from the lower half of the body and the left side of the upper body.\n\n5. Left subclavian vein: The thoracic duct empties into the left subclavian vein, which is a large vein that carries blood from the upper left side of the body back to the heart.\n\n6. Systemic circulation: Once the cancer cells have entered the left subclavian vein, they can travel through the systemic circulation to reach other parts of the body, including the lungs and brain.\n\nSo, the most likely lymphatic route for this malignancy to have metastasized to the brain is:\n\nB) Sentinel node - left lumbar trunk - cisterna chyli - thoracic duct - left subclavian vein - systemic circulation",
