                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nB) Gram-positive bacteria producing exotoxin that acts via ADP ribosylation\n\nThis presentation is consistent with diphtheria, which is caused by Corynebacterium diphtheriae, a gram-positive bacteria that produces an exotoxin that acts via ADP ribosylation. The grayish-white pseudomembrane in the oropharynx is a hallmark of the disease, and enlargement of cervical lymph nodes is also common. Yeast with pseudohyphae (A) is seen in fungal infections such as candidiasis, but would not present with these symptoms. Gram-positive cocci with hyaluronic acid capsule (C) describes Streptococcus pyogenes, which can cause pharyngitis, but does not produce a pseudomembrane or cervical lymph node enlargement. Gram-negative encapsulated bacteria producing IgA protease (D) describes Haemophilus influenzae, which can cause epiglottitis, but would not present with a pseudomembrane or cervical lymph node enlargement. Gram-negative bacteria producing exotoxin that inactivates elongation factor 2 (E) describes Pseudomonas aeruginosa, which can cause pneumonia, but would not present with a pseudomembrane or cervical lymph node enlargement.",
