                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Sub-questions:\n1. What is a type 1 error?\n2. How does a type 1 error occur?\n3. What would be the implications of a type 1 error in this study?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A type 1 error is a statistical error that occurs when the null hypothesis is rejected even though it is true.\n2. A type 1 error occurs when the significance level (alpha) is set too high, leading to a higher probability of rejecting the null hypothesis when it is actually true.\n3. If the authors had inadvertently encountered a type 1 error in this study, it would mean that they had falsely concluded that mepolizumab significantly reduced asthma exacerbations and improved markers of asthma control, when in fact there was no true difference between the study groups. This would be a serious error as it could lead to the adoption of an ineffective treatment and potentially harm patients. Therefore, it is important to carefully consider the significance level and conduct appropriate statistical analyses to minimize the risk of type 1 errors. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) A type 1 error occurs when the null hypothesis is true but is rejected in error.",
