                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Determine the mode of inheritance of the disease. The question does not provide this information, so we need to make an assumption. Let's assume that the disease is autosomal recessive, which means that an individual needs to inherit two copies of the disease-causing gene (one from each parent) to express the disease phenotype.\n\nStep 2: Determine the genotype of the female. The question tells us that she was born with the disease, which means that she has two copies of the disease-causing gene (homozygous). We can represent her genotype as dd.\n\nStep 3: Determine the genotype of the male. The question tells us that he does not have the disease phenotype, but we do not know his genotype. Let's represent his genotype as Dd, which means that he is a carrier of the disease-causing gene.\n\nStep 4: Determine the probability of each parent passing on their alleles to their offspring. Since the female has two copies of the disease-causing gene (dd), she can only pass on a d allele to her offspring. The male has one copy of the disease-causing gene (Dd), so he has a 50% chance of passing on a D allele and a 50% chance of passing on a d allele.\n\nStep 5: Use a Punnett square to determine the possible genotypes of the offspring. \n\n|   | D  | d  |\n|---|---|---|\n| d | Dd | dd |\n| d | Dd | dd |\n\nThere are four possible outcomes, with each offspring having a 50% chance of inheriting a d allele from the mother and a 50% chance of inheriting a D or d allele from the father.\n\nStep 6: Determine the probability of each offspring having the disease phenotype. To express the disease phenotype, an individual needs to inherit two copies of the disease-causing gene (dd). From the Punnett square, we can see that there is a 25% chance of each offspring inheriting two d alleles (dd) and therefore having the disease phenotype.\n\nStep 7: Calculate the overall probability of their child having the disease phenotype. Since there are four possible outcomes, each with a 25% chance of occurring, the overall probability of their child having the disease phenotype is 25% or 1/4.\n\nStep 8: Choose the answer that matches the calculated probability. The answer is A) 9/100, which is equivalent to 0.",
