                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Lyme disease is a tick-borne illness caused by the bacterium Borrelia burgdorferi. The disease is transmitted to humans through the bite of infected blacklegged ticks (Ixodes scapularis) and western blacklegged ticks (Ixodes pacificus). Symptoms can include a circular rash, fever, headache, muscle and joint aches, and facial drooping.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the patient's symptoms?\n- Sudden drooping of the right side of her face with drooling and excessive tearing\n- Inability to whistle or close the right eye\n- Circular red rash with central clearing on the trunk\n- Decreased taste sensation\n\n2. What is the likely diagnosis?\n- Lyme disease\n\n3. How is Lyme disease transmitted?\n- Through the bite of infected blacklegged ticks (Ixodes scapularis) and western blacklegged ticks (Ixodes pacificus)\n\n4. Which of the following most likely transmitted the organism responsible for this patient's illness?\n- Ixodes scapularis\n\nAnswer: Based on the patient's symptoms and the fact that she recently spent time outdoors in northern Maine, the likely diagnosis is Lyme disease. Lyme disease is transmitted through the bite of infected blacklegged ticks, with Ixodes scapularis being the most common vector in the northeastern United States. Therefore, the answer is A) Ixodes scapularis.",
