                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: \n- Newborns are at higher risk for infections due to their immature immune system.\n- Fever, irritability, and skin rash are common symptoms of various infections in newborns.\n- Acetaminophen is a medication used to reduce fever and pain.\n- Nikolsky's sign is a clinical sign that indicates the presence of a blistering skin disease.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the possible causes of fever, irritability, and skin rash in a 7-day-old newborn?\n2. What is scalded skin syndrome?\n3. What is Nikolsky's sign?\n4. What are the characteristic features of scalded skin syndrome?\n5. How is scalded skin syndrome diagnosed?\n6. What is the treatment for scalded skin syndrome?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The possible causes of fever, irritability, and skin rash in a 7-day-old newborn include bacterial infections (such as group B streptococcus, Escherichia coli, and Staphylococcus aureus), viral infections (such as herpes simplex virus and enteroviruses), and fungal infections (such as candidiasis).\n2. Scalded skin syndrome is a bacterial skin infection caused by Staphylococcus aureus that produces a toxin that causes the skin to peel off in sheets, similar to a burn.\n3. Nikolsky's sign is a clinical sign in which slight rubbing or pressure on the skin causes it to separate from the underlying tissue, indicating the presence of a blistering skin disease.\n4. The characteristic features of scalded skin syndrome include fever, irritability, diffuse erythema, and flaccid blisters that easily rupture and peel off, leaving a raw, moist surface.\n5. Scalded skin syndrome is diagnosed based on clinical presentation, bacterial culture of skin lesions, and blood tests to detect the presence of the toxin.\n6. Treatment for scalded skin syndrome includes antibiotics to treat the underlying bacterial infection, wound care to prevent infection and promote healing, and supportive care to manage fever and pain. In severe cases, hospitalization and intravenous fluids may be necessary. \n\nAnswer: B) Scalded skin syndrome is the most likely diagnosis in this patient based on the clinical presentation of fever, irritability, diffuse erythema, and flaccid blisters with a positive Nikolsky's sign. The history of trying to control the symptoms with acetaminophen and the presence of blisters around the butto",
