                        "cot": "\n\nB) Lichen sclerosus is the most likely diagnosis based on the patient's symptoms and physical examination findings. Lichen sclerosus is a chronic inflammatory skin condition that commonly affects the vulva and perianal area. It is characterized by white, thin, plaque-like lesions that can cause severe itching and pain. Atrophy of the labia minora and retraction of the clitoris are also common findings. Lichen sclerosus is more common in postmenopausal women and is associated with autoimmune disorders. Treatment typically involves topical corticosteroids and regular follow-up to monitor for potential complications such as squamous cell carcinoma. Squamous cell hyperplasia, squamous cell carcinoma of the vulva, lichen planus, and atrophic vaginitis are all possible differential diagnoses, but lichen sclerosus is the most likely based on the patient's presentation.",
