                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Squamous cell hyperplasia - This condition is characterized by thickened, white plaques on the vulvar skin, but it typically does not cause severe itching or pain during intercourse.\n\nB) Lichen sclerosus - This condition presents with severe itching, thinning of the skin, and white plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area. It can also cause pain during intercourse and changes in the appearance of the clitoris and labia minora. The perianal skin may also be affected.\n\nC) Squamous cell carcinoma of the vulva - This is a type of cancer that can cause itching and changes in the appearance of the vulvar skin, but it typically presents as a lump or ulcer rather than thin, white plaques.\n\nD) Lichen planus - This condition can cause itching and white plaques on the vulvar skin, but it typically affects other areas of the body as well, such as the mouth and nails.\n\nE) Atrophic vaginitis - This condition is caused by a decrease in estrogen levels and can cause itching, dryness, and pain during intercourse. However, it does not typically cause white plaque-like lesions or changes in the appearance of the clitoris and labia minora.\n\nBased on the patient's symptoms and physical examination findings, the most likely diagnosis is B) Lichen sclerosus.",
