                        "cot": "What is the most likely diagnosis for a 60-year-old woman with severe vulvar itching, dry, thin, white plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area, atrophy of the labia minora, retracted clitoris, and pale, wrinkled perianal skin?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The patient has severe vulvar itching that has been gradually worsening for the past year.\n- Over-the-counter lubricants do not provide substantial relief.\n- The patient has a medical history of diet-controlled type 2 diabetes mellitus and hypertension treated with hydrochlorothiazide.\n- Menopause occurred at 52 years of age.\n- The patient has been sexually active with her husband until recently and reports severe pain during vaginal intercourse.\n- The physical examination reveals dry, thin, white plaque-like lesions in the vulvar area with atrophy of the labia minora. The clitoris appears retracted. The perianal skin appears pale and wrinkled.\n- Based on these findings, the most likely diagnosis is lichen sclerosus.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) Lichen sclerosus.",
