                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the patient's medical history and current medications.\n- The patient has a history of diabetes and poorly controlled hypertension.\n- He takes enalapril and metformin.\n\nStep 2: Analyze the patient's vital signs and physical exam findings.\n- Temperature: 98.8\u00b0F (37.1\u00b0C)\n- Blood pressure: 155/90 mmHg (poorly controlled hypertension)\n- Pulse: 80/min\n- Respirations: 16/min\n\nStep 3: Determine the medication that was likely prescribed to the patient.\n- The physician added another anti-hypertensive medication to the patient's regimen.\n- One month later, the patient returns with new onset lower extremity swelling.\n- Lower extremity swelling is a common side effect of certain anti-hypertensive medications, particularly diuretics.\n- Among the options given, the only medication that is a diuretic is hydrochlorothiazide (option D).\n- Therefore, the likely medication that was prescribed to the patient is hydrochlorothiazide.\n\nAnswer: D) Hydrochlorothiazide",
