                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe sudden onset of severe bilateral vision loss with no perception of light suggests a problem with the optic nerve or the retina. The fact that the patient was able to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests that there is a problem with the left eye. This is known as a relative afferent pupillary defect (RAPD) and is a sign of optic nerve dysfunction.\n\nOption A) Optic neuritis is a condition where the optic nerve becomes inflamed, leading to vision loss. It can cause a RAPD, but it usually affects one eye more than the other. In this case, the patient has severe vision loss in both eyes.\n\nOption B) Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is a condition where the blood supply to the optic nerve is compromised, leading to vision loss. It can cause a RAPD, but it usually affects one eye more than the other. In this case, the patient has severe vision loss in both eyes.\n\nOption C) CMV retinitis is a viral infection that can cause vision loss, but it usually affects the retina rather than the optic nerve. The fact that the patient has a RAPD suggests that the problem is with the optic nerve rather than the retina.\n\nOption D) Functional vision loss is a condition where the patient experiences vision loss without any organic cause. However, the fact that the patient has a RAPD suggests that there is an organic cause for the vision loss.\n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis is A) Optic neuritis.",
                        "answers": [
