                    {
                        "id": "9156dfb3-545d-40a4-b0a4-114d044d1051",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The sudden onset of severe bilateral vision loss with no perception of light suggests a problem with the optic nerve. The fact that the loss is more severe on the right side also suggests a problem with the right optic nerve. The normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus suggest that the problem is not with the retina or the brain. The ability to touch the tips of her fingers with her right eye closed but not with her left eye closed suggests that the problem is with the left optic nerve. \n\nTherefore, the most likely diagnosis is optic neuritis, which is an inflammation of the optic nerve that can cause sudden vision loss, often more severe in one eye than the other. Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy and CMV retinitis are also possible causes of sudden vision loss, but they would be less likely given the normal exam findings. Functional vision loss is a possibility, but it would not explain the physical finding of the patient being able to touch the tips of her fingers with one eye closed but not the other.",
                        "answers": [
