                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, I have a question about the case you presented. What is the most likely diagnosis for the 25-year-old lady with sudden severe bilateral loss of vision?\n\nProfessor: The most likely diagnosis is one of the options given in the question. Can you tell me which one you think it is?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure, but I think it might be optic neuritis.\n\nProfessor: That's a good guess. Optic neuritis is inflammation of the optic nerve, which can cause sudden vision loss. But let's go through the other options to make sure we have the right answer.\n\nStudent: Okay, what about anterior ischemic optic neuropathy?\n\nProfessor: Anterior ischemic optic neuropathy is a condition where the blood flow to the optic nerve is reduced, causing damage and vision loss. However, this usually affects older individuals and is more common in those with high blood pressure or diabetes. The patient in this case is only 25 years old and there is no mention of any underlying medical conditions.\n\nStudent: I see. What about CMV retinitis?\n\nProfessor: CMV retinitis is a viral infection that can cause vision loss in people with weakened immune systems, such as those with HIV/AIDS. However, there is no mention of any immunodeficiency in this case, so it's unlikely to be the cause of the patient's symptoms.\n\nStudent: Okay, so it's probably not CMV retinitis. What about functional vision loss?\n\nProfessor: Functional vision loss is when a person experiences vision loss without any underlying physical cause. However, in this case, the patient has normal pupillary reflexes, fundus, and optokinetic nystagmus, which suggests that there is a physical cause for her vision loss.\n\nStudent: I understand. So, it's most likely optic neuritis?\n\nProfessor: Yes, based on the patient's symptoms and examination findings, optic neuritis is the most likely diagnosis. It's important to note that further testing, such as an MRI, may be needed to confirm the diagnosis.",
