                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n\n1. Understand what each option means.\n2. Determine which type of receptor has the most genes in the mammalian genome.\n3. Choose the correct answer.\n\nAnswer:\n\n1. A) Immunoglobulin receptors: These are proteins found on the surface of B cells that recognize and bind to specific antigens.\n2. B) Interleukins: These are signaling molecules that help regulate the immune response.\n3. C) Growth factors: These are proteins that stimulate cell growth and division.\n4. D) Odorants: These are molecules that bind to olfactory receptors in the nose, allowing us to detect different smells.\n\nTo determine which type of receptor has the most genes in the mammalian genome, we can look at scientific research and databases. According to the Human Genome Project, there are approximately 30,000 genes in the human genome. Of these, about 10% are thought to code for receptors. Within this group, the largest family of receptors is the G protein-coupled receptors (GPCRs), which includes receptors for hormones, neurotransmitters, and other signaling molecules. However, it is difficult to determine exactly how many genes code for each type of receptor, as there is still much to be learned about the human genome.\n\nBased on this information, we cannot definitively say which type of receptor has the most genes in the mammalian genome. Therefore, the answer to the question is: None of the above.",
