                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what is meant by \"mammalian genome\"?\n\nProfessor: The mammalian genome refers to the complete set of genetic material present in the cells of mammals, including humans.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what are genes?\n\nProfessor: Genes are segments of DNA that contain the instructions for making proteins, which are the building blocks of all living organisms.\n\nStudent: And what are receptors?\n\nProfessor: Receptors are proteins that are located on the surface of cells and are responsible for detecting and responding to specific molecules, such as hormones, neurotransmitters, or odorants.\n\nStudent: So, which type of receptors do the maximum number of genes code for in the mammalian genome?\n\nProfessor: The maximum number of genes in the mammalian genome code for the receptors of odorants, which are responsible for our sense of smell.\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. And what about the other options - immunoglobulin receptors, interleukins, and growth factors?\n\nProfessor: While these types of receptors are also important for various biological processes, they are not as numerous as odorant receptors in the mammalian genome. For example, immunoglobulin receptors are involved in the immune system, interleukins are signaling molecules involved in inflammation and immune responses, and growth factors are involved in cell growth and development.",
