                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Unfortunately, we cannot provide a specific answer without the figure mentioned in the question. However, we can give you some general information about the wave patterns of EEG, ECG, and EMG in different states.\n\nEEG (Electroencephalogram) measures brain activity and has different wave patterns for different states:\n- NREM sleep: Slow wave activity, including delta waves (0.5-4 Hz) and theta waves (4-8 Hz)\n- REM sleep: Low amplitude, mixed frequency pattern similar to wakefulness, with theta waves and occasional alpha waves (8-12 Hz)\n- Wakefulness: Alpha waves when relaxed with eyes closed, beta waves (12-30 Hz) when alert and focused\n- Quiet wakefulness: Alpha waves\n\nECG (Electrocardiogram) measures heart activity and does not have specific wave patterns for different sleep stages or wakefulness. It mainly shows the electrical activity of the heart, including the P wave, QRS complex, and T wave.\n\nEMG (Electromyogram) measures muscle activity and has different patterns for different states:\n- NREM sleep: Low muscle tone and activity\n- REM sleep: Muscle atonia (lack of muscle tone) with occasional muscle twitches\n- Wakefulness: Varies depending on muscle activity and movement\n- Quiet wakefulness: Low muscle tone and activity, similar to NREM sleep\n\nBased on this information, you may be able to deduce the correct answer if you can recall the specific wave patterns shown in the figure.",
