                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most effective test for evaluating intestinal malabsorption?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Intestinal malabsorption refers to the inability of the small intestine to absorb nutrients properly.\n- To assess this condition, we need a test that can measure the absorption of nutrients.\n- Option A, fecal fat estimation, is a commonly used test for malabsorption because it measures the amount of fat in the stool, which can indicate poor absorption of dietary fat.\n- Option B, serum lactose levels, is not a specific test for malabsorption, but it can be used to diagnose lactose intolerance, which can cause malabsorption.\n- Option C, serum amylase levels, is a test for pancreatic function, which can affect digestion but not necessarily absorption.\n- Option D, NBT-PABA test, is a test that measures the absorption of a synthetic compound that mimics folic acid, which can indicate malabsorption.\n- Based on this reasoning, option A, fecal fat estimation, is the best test for assessment of intestinal malabsorption.\n\nAnswer: A) Fecal fat estimation",
                        "answers": [
