                    {
                        "id": "55f373b8-76d3-437d-b9be-0f8bf2773f78",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-10",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, I would confirm that the patient has indeed been bitten by a dog and that the dog has not been vaccinated against rabies. Then, I would review the patient's medical history and perform a physical examination to assess the severity of the symptoms.\n\nNext, I would review the results of the corneal impression test. If Negri bodies are present, this would strongly suggest a diagnosis of rabies. However, if Negri bodies are not present, further testing would be necessary.\n\nBased on the current symptoms and test results, I would choose option A) Indirect immunofluorescence as the most accurate test for diagnosing rabies. This test detects antibodies against the rabies virus in the patient's blood or cerebrospinal fluid. It is highly sensitive and specific, and can detect the virus even in the early stages of infection.\n\nOption B) PCR for virus could also be used to detect the presence of the virus in the patient's blood or cerebrospinal fluid, but it may not be as accurate as the indirect immunofluorescence test.\n\nOption C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies is useful if Negri bodies are present in the corneal impression, but it is not as accurate as the other tests for diagnosing rabies.\n\nOption D) Antibodies against Rabies virus is similar to option A, but it is not as accurate as the indirect immunofluorescence test.",
                        "answers": [
