                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies.\n\nRabies is a viral infection that affects the central nervous system. It is transmitted through the saliva of infected animals, usually through a bite. The symptoms of rabies include hydrophobia (fear of water), tearing, altered sensorium, and eventually paralysis and death.\n\nCorneal impression is a diagnostic test used to detect the presence of the rabies virus in the patient's body. The test involves taking a sample of cells from the patient's cornea and examining them under a microscope.\n\nThe most accurate test for diagnosing rabies is the histopathological examination for Negri bodies. Negri bodies are characteristic inclusion bodies found in the cytoplasm of infected nerve cells. They are highly specific for rabies virus infection and can be detected in brain tissue or in corneal impressions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies.",
                        "answers": [
