                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What test should be done on a corneal impression from a patient with hydrophobia, tearing, and altered sensorium after a dog bite to accurately diagnose rabies?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. The patient has symptoms of hydrophobia, tearing, and altered sensorium after a dog bite.\n2. These symptoms are consistent with rabies.\n3. A corneal impression has been taken from the patient.\n4. The corneal impression can be used to diagnose rabies.\n5. We need to determine which test is most accurate for diagnosing rabies from a corneal impression.\n\nAnswer: C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies. This is the most accurate test for diagnosing rabies from a corneal impression. Negri bodies are characteristic inclusion bodies found in the cytoplasm of infected cells and are specific to rabies virus. Indirect immunofluorescence and antibodies against rabies virus can also be used for diagnosis, but they are less specific and sensitive than histopathological examination for Negri bodies. PCR for virus can also be used, but it may not be as accurate as histopathological examination.",
                        "answers": [
