                    {
                        "id": "0741be4f-c489-45d7-b748-69c713beb889",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Rabies is a viral disease that affects the central nervous system. It is transmitted through the saliva of infected animals, usually through a bite. Symptoms include hydrophobia (fear of water), tearing, altered sensorium, and eventually paralysis and death. Corneal impression is a diagnostic test that involves taking a sample of cells from the surface of the eye.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is indirect immunofluorescence?\n2. How does PCR for virus work?\n3. What are Negri bodies?\n4. How do antibodies against Rabies virus help diagnose the disease?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Indirect immunofluorescence is a diagnostic test that uses antibodies to detect the presence of a specific antigen (in this case, the Rabies virus) in a sample. The antibodies are labeled with a fluorescent dye, which allows them to be visualized under a microscope.\n2. PCR for virus is a diagnostic test that amplifies a specific segment of viral DNA or RNA in a sample. This allows for the detection of very small amounts of virus, even if it is present in low concentrations.\n3. Negri bodies are eosinophilic, cytoplasmic inclusions found in the neurons of animals infected with Rabies virus. They are a hallmark of the disease and can be seen on histopathological examination of brain tissue.\n4. Antibodies against Rabies virus can be detected in the blood of infected individuals using a variety of serological tests. These tests are useful for confirming a diagnosis of Rabies, but they may not be positive until several days after the onset of symptoms.\n\nAnswer: C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies is the most accurate test for diagnosing Rabies. While other tests may be useful for confirming a diagnosis, the presence of Negri bodies in brain tissue is a definitive sign of the disease.",
