                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what hydrophobia and altered sensorium mean in this context?\n\nProfessor: Hydrophobia is a symptom of rabies where the patient experiences difficulty in swallowing and has a fear of water. Altered sensorium refers to a change in the patient's mental state, such as confusion or delirium.\n\nStudent: Okay, so why is a corneal impression taken?\n\nProfessor: A corneal impression is taken to detect the presence of the rabies virus in the patient's tears. The virus can be present in the cornea and can be detected through this test.\n\nStudent: And what are the options for the test we can do on the corneal impression?\n\nProfessor: The options are A) Indirect immunofluorescence, B) PCR for virus, C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies, and D) Antibodies against Rabies virus.\n\nStudent: Which test is the most accurate for diagnosing rabies?\n\nProfessor: The most accurate test for diagnosing rabies is C) Histopathological examination for Negri bodies. This test involves examining brain tissue for the presence of Negri bodies, which are characteristic structures of the rabies virus. However, this test is invasive and requires a brain biopsy, which is not always feasible. \n\nStudent: If we can't do the histopathological examination, which test should we do?\n\nProfessor: In that case, we can do a combination of tests. The corneal impression can be tested using A) Indirect immunofluorescence or B) PCR for virus. We can also test the patient's blood for antibodies against the rabies virus (D). These tests can help confirm the diagnosis of rabies.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "5eb0ead7-30d5-455a-a3eb-9fad9a0cc065",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
