                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Measles is a highly contagious viral infection that spreads through the air when an infected person coughs or sneezes. It can cause serious complications, especially in young children and adults with weakened immune systems. Isolation is an important measure to prevent the spread of measles.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the prodromal stage of measles?\n2. What is the eruptive stage of measles?\n3. How long does the rash last in measles?\n4. When is a person with measles considered contagious?\n5. What is the recommended isolation period for measles?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The prodromal stage of measles is the initial phase of the infection, which lasts for 2-4 days. It is characterized by symptoms such as fever, cough, runny nose, and red eyes.\n2. The eruptive stage of measles is the phase when the characteristic rash appears. It usually starts on the face and spreads to the rest of the body. The rash lasts for about 5-6 days.\n3. The rash in measles lasts for about 5-6 days.\n4. A person with measles is considered contagious from 4 days before the rash appears until 4 days after the rash appears.\n5. The recommended isolation period for measles is from the onset of the prodromal stage until 4 days after the rash appears. Therefore, the correct answer is option A) Onset of prodromal stage until 7th day of rash.",
