                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the isolation period of measles is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the isolation period of measles is the time during which a person with measles is contagious and can spread the virus to others.\n\nStudent: What are the different stages of measles?\n\nProfessor: Measles has two main stages - the prodromal stage and the eruptive stage.\n\nStudent: What is the prodromal stage?\n\nProfessor: The prodromal stage is the first stage of measles, during which the person may experience symptoms such as fever, cough, runny nose, and red eyes.\n\nStudent: And when does the prodromal stage start?\n\nProfessor: The prodromal stage starts about 10-14 days after exposure to the virus.\n\nStudent: What about the eruptive stage?\n\nProfessor: The eruptive stage is the second stage of measles, during which the person develops a rash that spreads all over the body.\n\nStudent: And when does the eruptive stage start?\n\nProfessor: The eruptive stage starts about 2-4 days after the onset of the prodromal stage.\n\nStudent: So, what is the isolation period of measles?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is D) Eruptive stage until 7th day of rash. This means that a person with measles should be isolated from others from the time the rash appears until 7 days after the rash first appeared. During this time, the person can spread the virus to others, so it's important to take precautions to prevent the spread of the disease.",
