                        "cot": "A) PAX-6 is a gene that plays a role in eye development, but it is more commonly associated with aniridia, a condition where the iris is absent or underdeveloped.\n\nB) CRYGS-3 is not a recognized gene symbol. CRYGS is a gene that encodes a protein called gamma-S crystallin, which is involved in maintaining the transparency of the eye lens. Mutations in this gene have been associated with congenital cataracts.\n\nC) LMX1B is a gene that plays a role in limb and eye development. Mutations in this gene are associated with nail-patella syndrome, which can include ocular abnormalities, but not specifically congenital cataracts.\n\nD) PITX3 is a gene that is involved in eye development, and mutations in this gene have been associated with congenital cataracts, specifically anterior segment mesenchymal dysgenesis and congenital cataracts with microcornea.\n\nBased on this information, the gene most commonly indicated in congenital cataract is:\n\nAnswer: D) PITX-3",
