                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me which gene is commonly indicated in congenital cataract?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the gene commonly indicated in congenital cataract is one of the following: PAX-6, CRYGS-3, LMX-IB, or PITX-3.\n\nStudent: How do we know which one it is?\n\nProfessor: Well, studies have shown that PAX-6 is the most commonly mutated gene in congenital cataract, so that would be the most likely answer.\n\nStudent: Can you explain what PAX-6 does?\n\nProfessor: PAX-6 is a transcription factor that plays a critical role in eye development. It is involved in the formation of the lens and the retina, among other things.\n\nStudent: How does a mutation in PAX-6 lead to congenital cataract?\n\nProfessor: A mutation in PAX-6 can disrupt the normal development of the lens, leading to the formation of cataracts. This can happen in a number of ways, such as by affecting the differentiation of lens cells or by disrupting the normal structure of the lens.\n\nStudent: So, if someone has a mutation in PAX-6, does that mean they will definitely develop congenital cataract?\n\nProfessor: Not necessarily. While a mutation in PAX-6 is a risk factor for congenital cataract, it is not the only factor involved. Other genetic and environmental factors can also play a role in the development of cataracts.",
