                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nJaundice is a condition where the skin and whites of the eyes turn yellow due to the buildup of bilirubin in the blood. Bilirubin is a yellow pigment that is produced when red blood cells break down. It is normally processed by the liver and excreted in the bile. When the liver is unable to process bilirubin properly, it can lead to jaundice.\n\nIn this case, the direct bilirubin level is 50%, which means that there is a problem with the liver's ability to excrete bilirubin. The other liver function tests (LFTs) are normal, which rules out liver damage or inflammation.\n\nA) Rotor syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that causes jaundice, but it is usually associated with elevated levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin. This is not the correct answer.\n\nB) Gilbert syndrome is a common genetic disorder that causes mild jaundice, but it is usually associated with elevated levels of indirect bilirubin, not direct bilirubin. This is not the correct answer.\n\nC) Glucuronyl transferase deficiency is a rare genetic disorder that causes jaundice due to a deficiency in the enzyme that conjugates bilirubin in the liver. This can lead to elevated levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin. However, in this case, the direct bilirubin level is elevated while the other LFTs are normal, which is not consistent with this diagnosis. This is not the correct answer.\n\nD) Primary biliary cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease that can cause jaundice, but it is usually associated with elevated levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin, as well as other abnormalities in the LFTs. This is not the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is none of the above. Without more information, it is difficult to determine the exact cause of the jaundice in this case. Further testing and evaluation would be necessary to make a definitive diagnosis.",
