                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the diagnosis for a case of jaundice with 50% direct bilirubin and normal LFTs?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Jaundice is a yellowing of the skin and eyes due to high levels of bilirubin in the blood.\n- Bilirubin is a waste product from the breakdown of red blood cells.\n- There are two types of bilirubin: direct (conjugated) and indirect (unconjugated).\n- Direct bilirubin is water-soluble and can be excreted in the urine or feces, while indirect bilirubin is fat-soluble and needs to be processed by the liver before it can be excreted.\n- High levels of direct bilirubin suggest a problem with the liver's ability to process bilirubin.\n- LFTs (liver function tests) are blood tests that measure various enzymes and proteins produced by the liver.\n- Normal LFTs suggest that the liver is functioning normally, except for its ability to process bilirubin.\n- Rotor syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that causes jaundice and elevated levels of both direct and indirect bilirubin. It is unlikely to be the diagnosis in this case.\n- Gilbert syndrome is a common genetic disorder that causes mild jaundice and elevated levels of indirect bilirubin. It does not usually cause elevated levels of direct bilirubin, so it is unlikely to be the diagnosis in this case.\n- Glucuronyl transferase deficiency (also known as Crigler-Najjar syndrome) is a rare genetic disorder that causes severe jaundice and elevated levels of indirect bilirubin. It can also cause elevated levels of direct bilirubin in severe cases. However, since the LFTs are normal in this case, it is unlikely to be the diagnosis.\n- Primary biliary cirrhosis is a chronic liver disease that causes damage to the bile ducts, leading to elevated levels of direct bilirubin and other liver enzymes. However, since the LFTs are normal in this case, it is unlikely to be the diagnosis.\n\nAnswer: None of the options provided are a likely diagnosis based on the information given. Further testing and evaluation would be needed to determine the cause of the jaundice.",
