                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's list the symptoms and signs the patient is presenting with:\n- Fever\n- Fatigue\n- Jaundice\n- Yellow colored urine\n- Clay colored stools\n- Aversion to cigarette smoking\n- Scleral icterus\n- Abnormal LFT results\n\nThese symptoms and signs are suggestive of acute viral hepatitis. To rule out the different types of viral hepatitis, we need to test for the following markers:\n\n1. Hepatitis A: Anti-HAV IgM\n2. Hepatitis B: HBsAg and IgM anti-HBc\n3. Hepatitis C: Anti-HCV\n4. Hepatitis D: Anti-HDV (only if the patient is positive for Hepatitis B)\n5. Hepatitis E: Anti-HEV\n\nNow let's look at the options given:\n\nA) HBsAg, IgM antiHBC, AntiHCV AntiHEV\n- This option is missing Anti-HAV IgM, so it cannot rule out Hepatitis A.\n\nB) AntiHAV, HBsAg, IgM antiHBc, AntiHCV\n- This option is missing Anti-HEV, so it cannot rule out Hepatitis E.\n\nC) HBsAg, IgM antiHBC,AntiHDV AntiHCV AntiHEV\n- This option is missing Anti-HAV IgM, so it cannot rule out Hepatitis A.\n\nD) AntiHAV IgMantiHBC, AntiHCV, AntiHEV.\n- This option includes all the necessary markers to rule out Hepatitis A, B, C, and E. However, it does not include Anti-HDV, which is only necessary if the patient tests positive for Hepatitis B.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) AntiHAV IgMantiHBC, AntiHCV, AntiHEV. If the patient tests positive for Hepatitis B, then further testing for Anti-HDV should be done to rule out Hepatitis D.",
