                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Assess the severity of the child's condition and stabilize the airway if necessary.\n2. Obtain a detailed medical history and perform a physical examination to identify the cause of the symptoms.\n3. Based on the symptoms, consider the possible causes such as croup, epiglottitis, or bacterial tracheitis.\n4. Order appropriate diagnostic tests such as a throat culture, blood tests, or imaging studies to confirm the diagnosis.\n5. Initiate general airway management measures such as humidified oxygen, positioning, and suctioning if necessary.\n6. Administer nebulized racemic epinephrine if the child has croup.\n7. Administer corticosteroids to reduce inflammation and swelling of the airway.\n8. Administer IV antibiotics such as ceftriaxone if bacterial tracheitis or epiglottitis is suspected.\n9. Monitor the child's response to treatment and adjust the management plan accordingly.\n\nAnswer: C) Corticosteroids are recommended in the treatment of croup, epiglottitis, and bacterial tracheitis to reduce inflammation and swelling of the airway. Nebulized racemic epinephrine is also used in the treatment of croup. Anti-diphtheria toxin is not indicated in this case as the symptoms are not consistent with diphtheria. IV ceftriaxone may be used if bacterial tracheitis or epiglottitis is suspected.",
