                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The symptoms presented by the child suggest the possibility of acute epiglottitis, which is a medical emergency that requires prompt treatment to prevent airway obstruction.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is acute epiglottitis?\n2. What are the causes of acute epiglottitis?\n3. How is acute epiglottitis diagnosed?\n4. What is the general airway management for acute epiglottitis?\n5. What is the role of nebulized racemic epinephrine in the treatment of acute epiglottitis?\n6. What is the role of anti-diphtheria toxin in the treatment of acute epiglottitis?\n7. What is the role of corticosteroids in the treatment of acute epiglottitis?\n8. What is the role of IV ceftriaxone in the treatment of acute epiglottitis?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Acute epiglottitis is a bacterial infection of the epiglottis, which is a flap of tissue that covers the trachea during swallowing to prevent food or liquid from entering the airway.\n2. The most common cause of acute epiglottitis is infection with Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib) bacteria, but other bacteria such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Staphylococcus aureus, and group A streptococcus can also cause the condition.\n3. Acute epiglottitis is diagnosed based on the clinical presentation of high fever, inspiratory stridor, and difficulty swallowing, as well as visualization of a swollen and red epiglottis using a flexible laryngoscope.\n4. General airway management for acute epiglottitis involves maintaining the child in a position of comfort, providing supplemental oxygen, and avoiding any procedures that may cause agitation or distress, such as throat examination or intubation.\n5. Nebulized racemic epinephrine may be used to reduce airway swelling and improve breathing in children with acute epiglottitis, but it should be used with caution and only in a setting where advanced airway management is available.\n6. Anti-diphtheria toxin is not recommended for the treatment of acute epiglottitis, as the condition is not caused by the diphtheria bacteria.\n7. Corticosteroids such as dexamethasone may be used to reduce airway inflammation and improve",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "13c750ac-3c24-4228-8fc7-045a1dc3f718",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
