                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what inspiratory stridor means?\n\nProfessor: Sure, inspiratory stridor is a high-pitched sound that occurs during inhalation. It's caused by a narrowing of the upper airway, which can be due to inflammation, swelling, or a foreign object.\n\nStudent: Why would the child develop swallowing difficulty and drooling of saliva?\n\nProfessor: The difficulty swallowing and drooling of saliva are likely due to the narrowing of the upper airway. When the airway is narrowed, it can be difficult for the child to swallow and saliva can accumulate in the mouth.\n\nStudent: Why is general airway management not enough in this case?\n\nProfessor: General airway management, such as providing oxygen and ensuring the airway is clear, is important in any case of respiratory distress. However, in this case, the child's symptoms suggest a more severe narrowing of the upper airway that requires additional treatment.\n\nStudent: Can you explain the options for treatment?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the options are nebulized racemic epinephrine, anti-diphtheria toxin, corticosteroids, and IV ceftriaxone.\n\nStudent: What is nebulized racemic epinephrine?\n\nProfessor: Nebulized racemic epinephrine is a medication that can help reduce inflammation and swelling in the upper airway. It's delivered through a nebulizer, which turns the medication into a mist that the child can inhale.\n\nStudent: What is anti-diphtheria toxin?\n\nProfessor: Anti-diphtheria toxin is a medication that can be used to treat diphtheria, which is a bacterial infection that can cause respiratory distress. However, it's not typically used in cases of acute upper airway obstruction.\n\nStudent: What are corticosteroids?\n\nProfessor: Corticosteroids are a type of medication that can help reduce inflammation and swelling in the upper airway. They can be given orally or through an injection.\n\nStudent: What is IV ceftriaxone?\n\nProfessor: IV ceftriaxone is an antibiotic that can be used to treat bacterial infections. It's not typically used in cases of acute upper airway obstruction unless there is evidence of a bacterial infection. \n\nBased on the child's symptoms, nebulized racemic epinephrine would be the recommended treatment to help reduce inflammation and swelling in the upper airway. However, it's important to note that the underlying cause of the symptoms should also be investigated and treated if necessary",
                        "answers": [
