                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the symptoms presented by the child - high grade fever, inspiratory stridor, swallowing difficulty, and drooling of saliva.\n\nStep 2: Consider the possible causes of these symptoms. In this case, the symptoms suggest a possible upper airway obstruction, which could be caused by a variety of conditions such as croup, epiglottitis, or bacterial tracheitis.\n\nStep 3: Evaluate the treatment options listed in the question stem.\n\nA) Nebulized racemic epinephrine - This treatment is commonly used for croup, which is a viral infection that can cause upper airway obstruction. However, it may not be effective for other causes of upper airway obstruction.\n\nB) Anti-diphtheria toxin - This treatment is specific to diphtheria, which is a bacterial infection that can cause upper airway obstruction. However, it is not a common cause of upper airway obstruction in most settings.\n\nC) Corticosteroids - This treatment can help reduce inflammation in the upper airway and may be effective for a variety of causes of upper airway obstruction, including croup, epiglottitis, and bacterial tracheitis.\n\nD) IV ceftriaxone - This treatment is an antibiotic and would only be effective if the cause of the upper airway obstruction is a bacterial infection.\n\nStep 4: Based on the symptoms presented and the possible causes, the most appropriate treatment option would be corticosteroids (option C). This treatment can help reduce inflammation in the upper airway and may be effective for a variety of causes of upper airway obstruction. However, it is important to note that general airway management should also be initiated to ensure the child's airway is secure and oxygenation is maintained.",
