                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe target sign is a radiological finding seen on CT or MRI scans in cases of CSF rhinorrhea. It appears as a central area of low attenuation surrounded by a high attenuation ring. This is due to the presence of a CSF-filled sac surrounded by a rim of bone or soft tissue.\n\nNow, coming to the options:\n\nA) Spontaneous CSF Rhinorrhea: This refers to the leakage of CSF from the nose or ear without any history of trauma. It can be caused by various factors such as intracranial hypertension, tumors, or congenital defects. Therefore, it is possible to see the target sign in cases of spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea.\n\nB) Traumatic CSF Rhinorrhea: This refers to the leakage of CSF from the nose or ear due to a head injury or trauma. Again, it is possible to see the target sign in cases of traumatic CSF rhinorrhea.\n\nC) Both: As explained above, the target sign can be seen in both spontaneous and traumatic CSF rhinorrhea.\n\nD) None: This option is incorrect as the target sign is a well-known radiological finding in cases of CSF rhinorrhea.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Both.",
