                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Target sign refers to the appearance of a ring-like enhancement around a fluid collection on imaging studies.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is CSF rhinorrhea?\n2. What causes spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea?\n3. What causes traumatic CSF rhinorrhea?\n4. What imaging studies can show the target sign?\n5. Is the target sign seen in spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea?\n6. Is the target sign seen in traumatic CSF rhinorrhea?\n\nAnswers:\n1. CSF rhinorrhea is the leakage of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) from the nose due to a defect in the skull base or meninges.\n2. Spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea is caused by a spontaneous defect in the skull base or meninges, often due to increased intracranial pressure or a congenital abnormality.\n3. Traumatic CSF rhinorrhea is caused by a traumatic injury to the skull base or meninges, such as a fracture or penetrating injury.\n4. The target sign can be seen on imaging studies such as CT or MRI.\n5. The target sign can be seen in spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea.\n6. The target sign can also be seen in traumatic CSF rhinorrhea.\n\nAnswer: C) Both",
