                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain the question again, please?\n\nProfessor: Sure. The question is asking in which type of rhinorrhea, either spontaneous or traumatic, is the target sign seen?\n\nStudent: What is the target sign?\n\nProfessor: The target sign is a radiological finding seen on CT scans or MRI that indicates the presence of cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) in the nasal cavity. It appears as a round or oval-shaped lesion with a central hypodense area surrounded by a hyperdense rim.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what is spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea?\n\nProfessor: Spontaneous CSF rhinorrhea is when there is a leakage of CSF from the skull base into the nasal cavity without any history of trauma or surgery.\n\nStudent: And what is traumatic CSF rhinorrhea?\n\nProfessor: Traumatic CSF rhinorrhea is when there is a leakage of CSF from the skull base into the nasal cavity due to a traumatic injury or surgery.\n\nStudent: So, which type of rhinorrhea is the target sign seen in?\n\nProfessor: The target sign is seen in both spontaneous and traumatic CSF rhinorrhea. Therefore, the answer is C) Both.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that to me.",
