                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nTongue thrusting is a habit where the tongue pushes forward against the front teeth during swallowing or speaking. This can lead to dental problems such as malocclusion (misalignment of teeth) and speech difficulties. \n\nA) Spaced dentition: This can be seen in tongue thrusting as the constant pressure of the tongue can cause gaps between teeth. So, this feature is seen in tongue thrusting. \n\nB) Crowding and crossbite: Tongue thrusting can also cause crowding and crossbite as the tongue pushes against the teeth, causing them to shift out of position. So, this feature is seen in tongue thrusting. \n\nC) Open bite: Tongue thrusting can cause an open bite, where the front teeth do not touch when the back teeth are closed together. So, this feature is seen in tongue thrusting. \n\nD) Flush terminal plane: A flush terminal plane refers to the alignment of the last molars in the upper and lower jaws. It means that the molars are aligned in a straight line without any overlap or gaps. This feature is not related to tongue thrusting and is not seen in tongue thrusting. \n\nTherefore, the answer is D) Flush terminal plane.",
                        "answers": [
