                        "cot": "Tongue thrusting is a habit where the tongue pushes against the teeth or comes forward between the upper and lower teeth during swallowing, speaking, or at rest. It can cause dental problems such as malocclusion, speech difficulties, and difficulty chewing.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is spaced dentition?\n2. What is crowding and crossbite?\n3. What is an open bite?\n4. What is a flush terminal plane?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Spaced dentition refers to gaps or spaces between teeth.\n2. Crowding is when there is not enough space in the mouth for all the teeth, causing them to overlap or twist. Crossbite is when the upper teeth sit inside the lower teeth when biting down. \n3. An open bite is when the front teeth do not touch when biting down, leaving a gap between the upper and lower teeth.\n4. A flush terminal plane is a type of bite where the upper and lower back teeth meet evenly, without any overbite or underbite.\n\nAnswer:\nThe feature that is not seen in tongue thrusting is D) flush terminal plane. Tongue thrusting can cause spaced dentition, crowding and crossbite, and an open bite, but it does not affect the terminal plane.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f3e91707-8e2d-4074-aa72-3fe5ab32bcf4",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "D) Flush terminal plane.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
