                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what tongue thrusting is?\n\nProfessor: Tongue thrusting is a habit where the tongue pushes against the front teeth during swallowing or speaking.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what does spaced dentition mean?\n\nProfessor: Spaced dentition refers to gaps between teeth.\n\nStudent: And what about crowding and crossbite?\n\nProfessor: Crowding is when there isn't enough space for all the teeth to fit properly in the mouth, while crossbite is when the upper teeth sit inside the lower teeth.\n\nStudent: I see. And what is an open bite?\n\nProfessor: An open bite is when the front teeth don't touch when the back teeth are together.\n\nStudent: Got it. So which of these features is not seen in tongue thrusting?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is D) Flush terminal plane. This refers to the way the back teeth come together, and it is not affected by tongue thrusting.",
