                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain to me which type of natural disaster causes the maximum deaths?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the type of natural disaster that causes the maximum deaths is usually the one that affects the most people. Do you have any idea which type of natural disaster that might be?\n\nStudent: Hmm, I'm not sure. Can you give me some options?\n\nProfessor: Of course. There are four main types of natural disasters: hydrological, meteorological, geological, and fires. Which one do you think affects the most people?\n\nStudent: I'm not sure. Can you explain each one to me?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Hydrological disasters are caused by water, such as floods, tsunamis, and landslides. Meteorological disasters are caused by weather, such as hurricanes, tornadoes, and blizzards. Geological disasters are caused by the earth, such as earthquakes, volcanic eruptions, and avalanches. Fires are caused by heat and flames, such as wildfires and house fires.\n\nStudent: Okay, I think I understand. So which one causes the most deaths?\n\nProfessor: Well, it depends on the specific event and the location. However, in general, hydrological disasters tend to cause the most deaths because they can affect large areas and displace many people. For example, the 2004 Indian Ocean tsunami caused over 230,000 deaths.\n\nStudent: Wow, that's a lot. So just to clarify, the answer is A) Hydrological?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. Hydrological disasters cause the maximum deaths.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "c6bb136a-e689-4c1a-8178-59c192604c56",
