                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Alpha-fetoprotein (AFP) is a protein produced by the fetal liver and yolk sac during pregnancy. It is present in the fetal serum, placenta, amniotic fluid, and maternal serum. The levels of AFP vary in each of these compartments.\n\nA) Fetal serum: AFP levels are highest in the fetal serum, reaching a peak at around 12-16 weeks of gestation.\n\nB) Placenta: The placenta also produces AFP, but the levels are lower than those in the fetal serum.\n\nC) Amniotic fluid: AFP levels in the amniotic fluid are intermediate between those in the fetal serum and maternal serum. The levels increase throughout pregnancy, reaching a peak at around 32 weeks of gestation.\n\nD) Maternal serum: AFP levels in the maternal serum are the lowest among the four compartments. The levels increase during pregnancy, reaching a peak at around 32 weeks of gestation, but they are still much lower than those in the fetal serum and amniotic fluid.\n\nBased on this information, the maximum level of alpha-fetoprotein is seen in:\n\nA) Fetal serum",
