                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Metoprolol is a beta-blocker, which works by blocking the action of certain natural chemicals in your body, such as epinephrine, on the heart and blood vessels. This effect reduces heart rate, blood pressure, and strain on the heart.\n\nVerapamil is a calcium channel blocker, which works by relaxing the muscles of your heart and blood vessels. This helps to lower blood pressure and reduce the workload on the heart.\n\nWhen both of these medications are given together, they can have an additive effect on slowing down the heart rate and potentially causing a delay in the electrical conduction system of the heart.\n\nA) Atrial fibrillation is an irregular and often rapid heart rate that can increase the risk of stroke, heart failure, and other heart-related complications. This is not a likely outcome of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil.\n\nB) Bradycardia with AV Block is a slow heart rate and a delay or complete block in the electrical signals that control the heart's pumping action. This is a possible outcome of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil, as both medications can slow down the heart rate and affect the electrical conduction system.\n\nC) Torsades De pointes is a specific type of abnormal heart rhythm that can lead to sudden cardiac death. It is not a likely outcome of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil, as these medications do not typically cause this specific type of arrhythmia.\n\nD) Tachycardia is a fast heart rate, which is not a likely outcome of combining Metoprolol and Verapamil, as both medications work to slow down the heart rate.\n\nBased on this information, the most likely outcome of a patient with hypertension on Metoprolol and Verapamil is:\n\nAnswer: B) Bradycardia with AV Block",
