                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Metoprolol and Verapamil are both medications used to treat hypertension. Metoprolol is a beta-blocker that works by slowing down the heart rate and reducing the workload on the heart. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that relaxes the blood vessels and reduces the workload on the heart.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the mechanism of action of Metoprolol?\n2. What is the mechanism of action of Verapamil?\n3. What are the possible side effects of Metoprolol?\n4. What are the possible side effects of Verapamil?\n5. Can the combination of Metoprolol and Verapamil result in any adverse effects?\n\nAnswering the sub-questions:\n1. Metoprolol works by blocking the effects of adrenaline on the heart, which slows down the heart rate and reduces the workload on the heart.\n2. Verapamil works by blocking the entry of calcium into the heart and blood vessel cells, which relaxes the blood vessels and reduces the workload on the heart.\n3. The possible side effects of Metoprolol include fatigue, dizziness, low blood pressure, and slow heart rate.\n4. The possible side effects of Verapamil include constipation, dizziness, low blood pressure, and slow heart rate.\n5. Yes, the combination of Metoprolol and Verapamil can result in bradycardia with AV block, which is a slow heart rate with a delay in the electrical signals between the atria and ventricles.\n\nAnswer: B) Bradycardia with AV Block. The combination of Metoprolol and Verapamil can result in a slow heart rate with a delay in the electrical signals between the atria and ventricles, which is known as bradycardia with AV block.",
                        "answers": [
