                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what Metoprolol and Verapamil are used for?\n\nProfessor: Sure, Metoprolol is a beta-blocker that is used to treat hypertension and other cardiovascular conditions. Verapamil is a calcium channel blocker that is also used to treat hypertension and certain heart rhythm disorders.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what happens when a patient with hypertension is given both Metoprolol and Verapamil?\n\nProfessor: Well, both of these medications work to lower blood pressure and slow down the heart rate. When given together, they can have an additive effect, which means that the patient's blood pressure and heart rate may decrease even further.\n\nStudent: And what are the potential side effects of this combination?\n\nProfessor: One potential side effect is bradycardia with AV block, which means that the heart rate slows down too much and the electrical signals that control the heart's rhythm are disrupted. This can cause dizziness, fainting, and even cardiac arrest in severe cases.\n\nStudent: Are there any other potential side effects?\n\nProfessor: Yes, another potential side effect is atrial fibrillation, which is an irregular heart rhythm that can cause palpitations, shortness of breath, and chest pain. Additionally, in rare cases, the combination of Metoprolol and Verapamil can cause a type of irregular heart rhythm called Torsades De Pointes, which can be life-threatening.\n\nStudent: So, what is the most likely outcome if a patient with hypertension is given both Metoprolol and Verapamil?\n\nProfessor: The most likely outcome is bradycardia with AV block, but it's important to monitor the patient closely for any signs of other potential side effects. If any side effects do occur, the patient should seek medical attention immediately.",
