                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Down's syndrome is a genetic disorder caused by the presence of an extra chromosome 21. It is more common in pregnancies of older women.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is maternal alpha-feto protein (AFP)?\n2. How does AFP relate to Down's syndrome risk assessment?\n3. What is material HCG?\n4. How does HCG relate to Down's syndrome risk assessment?\n5. What is USG?\n6. How does USG relate to Down's syndrome risk assessment?\n7. What is chorionic villous biopsy?\n8. How does chorionic villous biopsy relate to Down's syndrome risk assessment?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Maternal alpha-feto protein (AFP) is a protein produced by the fetus and found in the mother's blood. \n2. High levels of AFP in the mother's blood can indicate a higher risk of neural tube defects or chromosomal abnormalities, including Down's syndrome.\n3. Material HCG is a hormone produced by the placenta during pregnancy. \n4. High levels of HCG in the mother's blood can also indicate a higher risk of chromosomal abnormalities, including Down's syndrome.\n5. USG (ultrasound) is a medical imaging technique that uses high-frequency sound waves to create images of the inside of the body. \n6. An ultrasound can detect physical markers of Down's syndrome, such as an increased thickness of the nuchal fold (the skin at the back of the baby's neck).\n7. Chorionic villous biopsy is a prenatal test that involves taking a small sample of the placenta to test for chromosomal abnormalities. \n8. Chorionic villous biopsy can provide a definitive diagnosis of Down's syndrome, but it carries a small risk of miscarriage.\n\nTo assess the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy, a combination of maternal AFP and HCG levels, ultrasound, and/or chorionic villous biopsy may be used. The specific approach will depend on the preferences of the patient and healthcare provider, as well as the availability of resources and expertise.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8f6d8f99-aa6b-4f9c-bdd6-25cb98748982",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "that all of them can be used to assess the risk of Down's syndrome in the present pregnancy, but the specific approach will depend on individual circumstances.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
