                        "cot": "\n\nOption A, obstinate cry, implies a stubborn or defiant cry, which does not fit the description of a constant whining noise without tears or sobs.\n\nOption B, frighten cry, suggests a cry caused by fear or terror, which may involve tears and sobs, but not necessarily a constant whining noise.\n\nOption C, hurt cry, may involve tears and sobs, but again, not necessarily a constant whining noise.\n\nOption D, compensatory cry, refers to a cry that occurs after a period of quiet, such as when a baby takes a breath after holding it in for a while. This type of cry does not fit the description of a constant whining noise without tears or sobs.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is none of the above. There is no specific term for a cry that only involves a constant whining noise without tears or sobs.",
