                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Babies communicate through crying, and different types of cries can indicate different needs or emotions. It is important for caregivers to be able to distinguish between different types of cries in order to respond appropriately.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are some common reasons for a baby to cry?\n2. What are some physical and behavioral cues that can help identify different types of cries?\n3. What is the definition of each of the four options given?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Common reasons for a baby to cry include hunger, discomfort (such as a dirty diaper or clothing that is too tight), tiredness, overstimulation, illness or pain, and a need for attention or comfort.\n2. Physical cues that can help identify different types of cries include the pitch, duration, and intensity of the cry, as well as the baby's facial expressions, body movements, and breathing patterns. Behavioral cues may include whether the baby is sucking on their hands or fingers, arching their back, or pulling their legs up to their chest.\n3. A) Obstinate cry: a cry that is stubborn or resistant, often accompanied by tense body language. B) Frighten cry: a cry that is sudden and sharp, often accompanied by a startle reflex and wide eyes. C) Hurt cry: a cry that is high-pitched and intense, often accompanied by a grimace or frown. D) Compensatory cry: a cry that is used to make up for a lack of crying in the past, often seen in premature babies or those with medical issues. \nBased on this information, the answer to the original question is A) Obstinate cry.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "f7cff711-32be-4967-b49d-84bbe87c7555",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Obstinate cry, which is a cry that is stubborn or resistant, often accompanied by tense body language. It is a type of cry that does not involve tears or sobs, but rather a constant whining noise. Caregivers should pay attention to the physical and behavioral cues that accompany this type of cry in order to identify the underlying need or emotion and respond appropriately.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
