                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the most likely diagnosis for a 50-year-old male who presents with ptosis, difficulty in chewing, and occasional difficulty in swallowing, but no history of diplopia or visual loss, and has symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg, a decremental response in orbicularis only on nerve conduction study, and a myopathic pattern on ERG, with a negative Anti-AchR radioimmunoassay?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The symptoms of ptosis, difficulty in chewing, and occasional difficulty in swallowing suggest a neuromuscular disorder affecting the muscles involved in these functions.\n- The absence of diplopia or visual loss suggests that the disorder is not affecting the eye muscles specifically.\n- The symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg suggest a generalized muscle weakness rather than a localized problem.\n- The decremental response in orbicularis only on nerve conduction study suggests a problem with neuromuscular transmission, which is a characteristic feature of myasthenia gravis.\n- The myopathic pattern on ERG further supports a neuromuscular disorder affecting muscle function.\n- The negative Anti-AchR radioimmunoassay rules out the most common form of myasthenia gravis, which is caused by autoantibodies against the acetylcholine receptor.\n- Therefore, the most probable diagnosis would be ocular myasthenia gravis, which is a subtype of myasthenia gravis that primarily affects the eye muscles but can also involve other muscles.\n\nAnswer: A) Ocular myasthenia gravis",
                        "answers": [
