                    {
                        "id": "af1eac06-9f00-44b8-95b7-e9cbc4cadd4b",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disorder that affects the neuromuscular junction, leading to muscle weakness and fatigue. It can be classified as ocular or generalized, depending on the extent of muscle involvement. Ocular myasthenia gravis primarily affects the muscles that control eye movement and eyelid function, while generalized myasthenia gravis affects multiple muscle groups throughout the body. \n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the patient's symptoms?\n- Ptosis, difficulty in chewing, occasional difficulty in swallowing\n2. What is the patient's age and gender?\n- 50-year-old male\n3. What is the result of the finger abduction test?\n- Mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg\n4. What is the result of the nerve conduction study?\n- Decremental response in orbicularis only\n5. What is the result of the ERG?\n- Myopathic pattern\n6. What is the result of the anti-AchR radioimmunoassay?\n- Negative\n\nAnswers:\n1. The patient's symptoms include ptosis, difficulty in chewing, and occasional difficulty in swallowing.\n2. The patient is a 50-year-old male.\n3. The finger abduction test shows mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement.\n4. The nerve conduction study shows decremental response in orbicularis only.\n5. The ERG reveals a myopathic pattern.\n6. The anti-AchR radioimmunoassay is negative.\n\nBased on the information provided, the most probable diagnosis would be ocular myasthenia gravis (A), as the patient's symptoms and test results suggest involvement of the muscles controlling eye movement and eyelid function, without significant involvement of other muscle groups. The negative anti-AchR radioimmunoassay does not rule out the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis, as up to 10-15% of patients with myasthenia gravis may be seronegative for anti-AchR antibodies. Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (D) is less likely, as it typically presents with progressive weakness of the extraocular muscles and ptosis, without involvement of other muscle groups or myopathic changes on ERG.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "4dc7380e-aeed-4be5-bce3-ab628f8fb318",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Ocular myasthenia gravis.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
