                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Read the question and the given information carefully.\n\nA 50-year-old male presented with complains of ptosis, difficulty in chewing and occasional difficulty in swallowing. There is no history of diplopia or visual loss. On examination, there is symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg. Nerve conduction study shows decremental response in orbicularis only. ERG revealed a myopathic pattern. Anti-AchR radioimmunoassay was negative.\n\nStep 2: Understand the key terms and concepts.\n\nPtosis: drooping of the upper eyelid\nDifficulty in chewing: difficulty in moving the jaw muscles\nDifficulty in swallowing: difficulty in moving the throat muscles\nDiplopia: double vision\nSymmetric ptosis: drooping of both eyelids equally\nExtraocular muscle movement: movement of the muscles that control eye movement\nFinger abduction test: a test to assess the strength of the muscles that move the eyes\nNerve conduction study: a test to assess the function of the nerves that control muscle movement\nDecremental response: a decrease in muscle response with repeated nerve stimulation\nOrbicularis: a muscle that controls eyelid closure\nERG: electroretinogram, a test to assess the function of the retina\nMyopathic pattern: a pattern of muscle dysfunction seen in muscle diseases\nAnti-AchR radioimmunoassay: a test to detect antibodies against acetylcholine receptors, which are often present in myasthenia gravis\n\nStep 3: Identify the possible diagnoses based on the given information.\n\nThe patient has ptosis, difficulty in chewing, and occasional difficulty in swallowing, which suggest muscle weakness. The symmetric ptosis and mild restriction of extraocular muscle movement with finger abduction test 60deg suggest a neuromuscular disorder affecting the muscles that control eye movement. The nerve conduction study shows a decremental response in orbicularis only, which is a characteristic finding in myasthenia gravis. However, the anti-AchR radioimmunoassay was negative, which makes the diagnosis of myasthenia gravis less likely. The ERG revealed a myopathic pattern, which suggests a muscle disease. Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO) is a muscle disease that affects the muscles that control eye movement and can cause ptosis, but it usually presents with a slow, progressive onset and is more common in older adults. Ocular my",
