                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "A) Roth spot - Roth spots are retinal hemorrhages with a pale center, which can be a feature of infective endocarditis.\n\nB) Osler's nodes - Osler's nodes are painful, red nodules on the fingers and toes, which can be a feature of infective endocarditis.\n\nC) Aschoff nodules - Aschoff nodules are granulomatous lesions found in the heart tissue, typically associated with rheumatic fever, not infective endocarditis.\n\nD) Positive blood culture - Positive blood culture is a common finding in infective endocarditis, as it indicates the presence of bacteria in the bloodstream.\n\nSo, the correct answer is C) Aschoff nodules, as they are not a feature of infective endocarditis.",
