                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Define infective endocarditis.\n2. List the common features of infective endocarditis.\n3. Identify which feature is not commonly associated with infective endocarditis.\n\nAnswer:\n1. Infective endocarditis is an infection of the inner lining of the heart chambers and heart valves.\n2. Common features of infective endocarditis include fever, chills, fatigue, heart murmur, petechiae, splinter hemorrhages, Roth spot, Osler's nodes, Janeway lesions, and positive blood culture.\n3. Aschoff nodules are not commonly associated with infective endocarditis. Aschoff nodules are a feature of rheumatic fever, which is a different condition. \n\nTherefore, the answer is C) Aschoff nodules.",
