                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Infective endocarditis is an infection of the inner lining of the heart chambers and valves. It can present with various symptoms and signs. The question asks us to identify the feature that is not associated with infective endocarditis. Let's examine each option:\n\nA) Roth spot: This is a retinal lesion that appears as a white spot with a hemorrhagic center. It is caused by emboli from infective endocarditis. Therefore, Roth spot is a feature of infective endocarditis.\n\nB) Osler's nodes: These are painful nodules that appear on the fingers and toes. They are caused by immune complex deposition in the blood vessels. Osler's nodes are a classic feature of infective endocarditis.\n\nC) Aschoff nodules: These are granulomatous lesions that appear in the heart muscle. They are a feature of rheumatic fever, not infective endocarditis.\n\nD) Positive blood culture: This is a definitive diagnostic test for infective endocarditis. Therefore, positive blood culture is a feature of infective endocarditis.\n\nBased on the above analysis, the answer is C) Aschoff nodules.",
