                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Infective endocarditis is an infection of the inner lining of the heart chambers and heart valves. It is caused by bacteria or fungi that enter the bloodstream and attach to damaged areas of the heart.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a Roth spot?\n2. What are Osler's nodes?\n3. What are Aschoff nodules?\n4. Is a positive blood culture a feature of infective endocarditis?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A Roth spot is a retinal hemorrhage with a white center that is seen in some cases of infective endocarditis. Therefore, Roth spot is a feature of infective endocarditis.\n2. Osler's nodes are painful, red, raised lesions on the fingers and toes that are seen in some cases of infective endocarditis. Therefore, Osler's nodes are a feature of infective endocarditis.\n3. Aschoff nodules are small, inflammatory nodules that are seen in the heart muscle in some cases of rheumatic fever, not infective endocarditis. Therefore, Aschoff nodules are not a feature of infective endocarditis.\n4. A positive blood culture is a diagnostic criterion for infective endocarditis. Therefore, a positive blood culture is a feature of infective endocarditis.\n\nTherefore, the answer to the original question is C) Aschoff nodules.",
