                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Pyloric stenosis is a condition in which the muscle at the bottom of the stomach (pylorus) thickens and blocks food from entering the small intestine. It is more common in males and typically presents with projectile vomiting, weight loss, and dehydration. Hypocalcemia (low serum calcium levels) can occur in neonates with pyloric stenosis due to vomiting and loss of stomach acid.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. Why is it important to know the neonate's blood glucose level before supplementing calcium?\n2. How does serum protein level relate to calcium supplementation in neonates?\n3. Can serum bilirubin level affect calcium supplementation in neonates?\n4. What is the relevance of oxygen saturation in calcium supplementation for neonates with pyloric stenosis?\n\nAnswers:\n1. It is important to know the neonate's blood glucose level before supplementing calcium because hypocalcemia can cause hypoglycemia (low blood sugar). If the neonate already has low blood sugar, supplementing calcium could worsen the hypoglycemia.\n2. Serum protein level can affect calcium supplementation in neonates because calcium binds to protein in the blood. If the neonate has low serum protein levels, there may be less protein available to bind with the supplemented calcium, leading to an increased risk of hypocalcemia.\n3. Serum bilirubin level does not directly affect calcium supplementation in neonates with pyloric stenosis. However, high bilirubin levels can indicate liver dysfunction, which can affect the metabolism and excretion of calcium. Therefore, it may be important to investigate the cause of high bilirubin levels before supplementing calcium.\n4. Oxygen saturation is not directly related to calcium supplementation in neonates with pyloric stenosis. However, if the neonate is experiencing severe vomiting and dehydration, their oxygen saturation may be affected. In this case, it may be necessary to address the dehydration and hypovolemia before supplementing calcium.",
