                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Understand the anatomy of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ) and the muscles involved in its movement.\n2. Identify the direction of lateral movement of the condyle.\n3. Determine which muscle(s) are responsible for this movement.\n4. Choose the option that corresponds to the muscle(s) responsible for lateral movement of the condyle.\n\nAnswer:\n1. The TMJ is a hinge joint that connects the mandible (lower jaw) to the temporal bone of the skull. It is responsible for the movement of the jaw, including opening and closing, protrusion and retrusion, and lateral movement.\n2. Lateral movement of the condyle refers to the movement of the mandibular condyle (the rounded end of the mandible that articulates with the temporal bone) away from the midline of the body, towards either the left or right side.\n3. The lateral pterygoid muscle is responsible for lateral movement of the condyle. There are two lateral pterygoid muscles, one on each side of the jaw. When they contract, they pull the mandible forward and to the side, causing lateral movement of the condyle.\n4. Therefore, the correct answer is A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid, as the muscle on the opposite side of the jaw is responsible for lateral movement of the condyle.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "31e0a92e-aa3f-41ee-8499-d321d66bbd4a",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
