                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The question is asking about the muscle responsible for lateral movement of the condyle. \n\nTo answer this question, we need to understand the anatomy of the temporomandibular joint (TMJ). The TMJ is a synovial joint that connects the mandible (lower jaw) to the temporal bone of the skull. The joint is made up of two main parts: the condyle of the mandible and the glenoid fossa of the temporal bone. \n\nLateral movement of the condyle occurs when the mandible moves sideways, away from the midline of the body. This movement is important for activities such as chewing and speaking. \n\nNow, let's consider the answer choices:\n\nA) Contralateral lateral pterygoid: This means the lateral pterygoid muscle on the opposite side of the body from the condyle is responsible for lateral movement. However, this is not correct because the lateral pterygoid muscle on the same side as the condyle is actually responsible for lateral movement.\n\nB) Ispilateral lateral pterygoid: This means the lateral pterygoid muscle on the same side of the body as the condyle is responsible for lateral movement. This is the correct answer because the lateral pterygoid muscle is the primary muscle responsible for lateral movement of the condyle.\n\nC) Same side medial pterygoid: The medial pterygoid muscle is not responsible for lateral movement of the condyle. It is involved in elevating the mandible and moving it forward.\n\nD) None of the above: This is not correct because answer choice B is the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Ispilateral lateral pterygoid.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "40b036eb-9b47-48fe-a9ae-d4626e28e787",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
