                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To clarify, the question is asking which muscle is responsible for the lateral movement of the condyle in the temporomandibular joint.\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- The temporomandibular joint is the joint that connects the jawbone to the skull.\n- The condyle is the rounded end of the jawbone that fits into the socket of the skull.\n- Lateral movement refers to movement of the condyle to the side.\n- The lateral pterygoid muscles are located on both sides of the jaw and are responsible for moving the jaw side to side.\n- The contralateral lateral pterygoid refers to the muscle on the opposite side of the jaw from the direction of movement.\n- The ipsilateral lateral pterygoid refers to the muscle on the same side of the jaw as the direction of movement.\n- The medial pterygoid muscles are also located on both sides of the jaw and are responsible for elevating the jaw.\n- Therefore, the muscle responsible for the lateral movement of the condyle is either the contralateral or ipsilateral lateral pterygoid muscle.\n\nAnswer: A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid",
