                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The temporomandibular joint (TMJ) is a hinge joint that connects the jawbone to the skull. It allows for movements such as opening and closing the mouth, as well as side-to-side movements.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is lateral movement of the condyle?\n2. What are the lateral pterygoid and medial pterygoid muscles?\n3. How do the lateral pterygoid and medial pterygoid muscles contribute to TMJ movement?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Lateral movement of the condyle refers to the movement of the jawbone to the side, away from the midline of the body.\n2. The lateral pterygoid muscle is a muscle located in the TMJ region that is responsible for opening the mouth and moving the jawbone from side to side. The medial pterygoid muscle is another muscle in the TMJ region that is responsible for closing the mouth and moving the jawbone from side to side.\n3. The lateral pterygoid muscle is responsible for contralateral movement of the condyle, meaning that it moves the jawbone to the opposite side of the muscle. Therefore, option A is correct. The ipsilateral lateral pterygoid muscle would move the jawbone to the same side as the muscle, so option B is incorrect. The same side medial pterygoid muscle would not contribute to lateral movement of the condyle, so option C is incorrect. Therefore, the correct answer is A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid.",
