                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what lateral movement of condyle means?\n\nProfessor: Sure, lateral movement of condyle refers to the movement of the mandibular condyle (the rounded end of the lower jaw bone) away from the midline of the skull.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what does contralateral mean?\n\nProfessor: Contralateral means the opposite side. So, in this case, it would mean the lateral pterygoid muscle on the opposite side of the mandible from the direction of movement.\n\nStudent: And what about ipsilateral?\n\nProfessor: Ipsilateral means the same side. So, in this case, it would mean the lateral pterygoid muscle on the same side of the mandible as the direction of movement.\n\nStudent: What does the medial pterygoid muscle do?\n\nProfessor: The medial pterygoid muscle is responsible for elevating the mandible (closing the mouth) and moving it from side to side.\n\nStudent: So, if the lateral movement of the condyle is happening on the same side as the medial pterygoid muscle, does that mean it's the answer?\n\nProfessor: No, actually, the correct answer is A) Contralateral lateral pterygoid. This muscle is responsible for pulling the mandible in the opposite direction of the movement, allowing for lateral movement of the condyle.",
