                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Ewing's postulates concern with growth after a neurological injury. \n\nEwing's postulates were developed by James Ewing in 1928 to establish a set of criteria for determining whether a tumor is malignant or not. These postulates include the following:\n\n1. The tumor must be removed intact and examined microscopically.\n2. The tumor must be transplanted into a suitable host and produce a similar tumor.\n3. The tumor cells must be able to be cultured in vitro.\n4. The tumor must be able to be induced in an animal by injecting the cultured cells.\n\nWhile Ewing's postulates were originally developed for tumors, they have also been applied to other areas of research, including growth after neurological injury. In this context, Ewing's postulates are used to establish whether a particular treatment or intervention is effective in promoting growth and regeneration after injury.",
