                    {
                        "id": "ca2fd23b-49de-48fd-b0ff-b68d0f58cf3c",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The child presents with chronic liver failure, muscle weakness, low serum glucose, ketoacidosis, decreased pH, and raised ALT and AST levels. These symptoms suggest a glycogen storage disease, which is a group of inherited metabolic disorders that result from defects in enzymes involved in glycogen metabolism.\n\nThe normal blood lactate and uric acid levels help us rule out some glycogen storage diseases. The fact that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast suggests a defect in the pathway of glycogen breakdown.\n\nLiver biopsy shows increased glycogen in the liver, which further supports the diagnosis of a glycogen storage disease. Now let's analyze the enzyme options:\n\nA) Glucose-6-phosphatase: This enzyme is involved in the final step of glycogenolysis, converting glucose-6-phosphate to glucose. A deficiency in this enzyme would lead to glycogen storage disease type I (Von Gierke's disease), which is characterized by hypoglycemia, lactic acidosis, and hepatomegaly. This option seems to fit the clinical presentation of the child.\n\nB) Muscle phosphorylase: This enzyme is involved in glycogen breakdown in muscles. A deficiency in this enzyme would lead to glycogen storage disease type V (McArdle's disease), which is characterized by exercise intolerance and muscle cramps. This option does not fit the clinical presentation of the child.\n\nC) Branching enzyme: This enzyme is involved in glycogen synthesis. A deficiency in this enzyme would lead to glycogen storage disease type IV (Andersen's disease), which is characterized by progressive liver cirrhosis and neurological abnormalities. This option does not fit the clinical presentation of the child.\n\nD) Debranching enzyme: This enzyme is involved in glycogen breakdown. A deficiency in this enzyme would lead to glycogen storage disease type III (Cori's disease), which is characterized by hepatomegaly, hypoglycemia, and muscle weakness. This option seems to fit the clinical presentation of the child, but the response to glucagon after meals suggests that glycogenolysis is still partially functional, which is not consistent with a debranching enzyme deficiency.\n\nBased on the clinical presentation and the response to glucagon, the most likely enzyme to be defective in this child is:\n\nA) Glucose-6-phosphatase",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "3be8d334-30db-4359-8314-ee2f558fdb83",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Glucose-6-phosphatase.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
