                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Identify the symptoms and laboratory findings.\n2. Understand the role of enzymes in glucose metabolism.\n3. Analyze the liver biopsy report.\n4. Match the enzyme defect with the symptoms and laboratory findings.\n\nAnswer: A) Glucose-6-phosphatase\n\nExplanation:\n1. The child has chronic liver failure and muscle weakness at 3 months of age. Serum glucose is low, along with ketoacidosis and decreased pH. ALT and AST are raised. Blood lactate and uric acid levels are normal. Intravenous glucagon given after meals raises the blood glucose levels, but does not raise glucose when given after an overnight fast.\n2. Glucose-6-phosphatase is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in glucose metabolism. It catalyzes the hydrolysis of glucose-6-phosphate to glucose and inorganic phosphate, which is the final step in gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis. Deficiency of this enzyme leads to impaired glucose homeostasis and glycogen storage disease type I (GSD-I).\n3. The liver biopsy shows increased glycogen in the liver, which is a characteristic feature of GSD-I.\n4. The symptoms and laboratory findings are consistent with GSD-I, which is caused by a defect in glucose-6-phosphatase. Therefore, the enzyme likely to be defective in this child is glucose-6-phosphatase.",
