                    {
                        "id": "3c6adad4-a3f9-4687-aa5f-eaa45121dfa1",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Glycogen is a storage form of glucose in the body, primarily stored in the liver and muscles. Glucose-6-phosphatase is an enzyme that plays a crucial role in glucose homeostasis by converting glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which can then be released into the bloodstream. Muscle phosphorylase is an enzyme that breaks down glycogen in muscle tissue to release glucose for energy. Branching enzyme and debranching enzyme are involved in the synthesis and breakdown of glycogen.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the symptoms and findings in this child?\n- Chronic liver failure and muscle weakness at 3 months of age\n- Low serum glucose, ketoacidosis, and decreased pH\n- Raised ALT and AST\n- Normal blood lactate and uric acid levels\n- Intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast\n- Increased glycogen in liver on biopsy\n\n2. What is the role of glucose-6-phosphatase in glucose homeostasis?\n- Converts glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which can be released into the bloodstream\n- Plays a crucial role in maintaining blood glucose levels\n\n3. What is the role of muscle phosphorylase in glycogen breakdown?\n- Breaks down glycogen in muscle tissue to release glucose for energy\n\n4. What is the role of branching enzyme and debranching enzyme in glycogen synthesis and breakdown?\n- Branching enzyme adds branches to glycogen molecules during synthesis\n- Debranching enzyme removes branches from glycogen molecules during breakdown\n\nAnswer:\nBased on the symptoms and findings in this child, along with the increased glycogen in the liver and the fact that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast, the likely enzyme defect is glucose-6-phosphatase. This is because glucose-6-phosphatase plays a crucial role in maintaining blood glucose levels by converting glucose-6-phosphate to glucose, which can be released into the bloodstream. The other enzymes listed (muscle phosphorylase, branching enzyme, and debranching enzyme) are not directly involved in glucose homeostasis and would not explain the low serum glucose levels and other findings in this child.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "47bca049-dd1e-4ace-9669-7c23ab2645c6",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Glucose-6-phosphatase.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
