                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what chronic liver failure and muscle weakness mean in this context?\n\nProfessor: Chronic liver failure means that the liver is not functioning properly for an extended period of time. Muscle weakness refers to a decrease in muscle strength and tone.\n\nStudent: What does it mean when the serum glucose is low and there is ketoacidosis and decreased pH?\n\nProfessor: Low serum glucose means that there is not enough glucose in the blood. Ketoacidosis is a condition where the body produces high levels of ketones, which can be harmful. Decreased pH means that the blood is more acidic than normal.\n\nStudent: What do the raised ALT and AST levels indicate?\n\nProfessor: ALT and AST are enzymes that are normally found in the liver. When the liver is damaged, these enzymes can leak into the bloodstream, causing their levels to rise.\n\nStudent: Why are blood lactate and uric acid levels normal?\n\nProfessor: Blood lactate and uric acid levels are not typically affected in this condition. They are not specific markers for liver or muscle dysfunction.\n\nStudent: What does it mean that intravenous glucagon raises blood glucose levels after meals but not after an overnight fast?\n\nProfessor: Glucagon is a hormone that helps to raise blood glucose levels. After meals, the body releases insulin to lower blood glucose levels. Glucagon can counteract the effects of insulin and raise blood glucose levels. However, after an overnight fast, there is no insulin in the bloodstream, so glucagon alone cannot raise blood glucose levels.\n\nStudent: What does increased glycogen in the liver indicate?\n\nProfessor: Glycogen is a storage form of glucose in the liver. Increased glycogen in the liver can indicate a problem with the enzymes that regulate glycogen metabolism.\n\nStudent: So, which enzyme is likely to be defective in this child?\n\nProfessor: Based on the symptoms and findings, the most likely enzyme to be defective in this child is glucose-6-phosphatase. This enzyme is responsible for releasing glucose from glycogen in the liver. When it is defective, glycogen accumulates in the liver and glucose cannot be released into the bloodstream, leading to low blood glucose levels.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "364db7d4-d2ca-4f75-b746-2b444d283f77",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Glucose-6-phosphatase.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
