                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain the question again?\n\nProfessor: Sure. An autopsy was done on a person who died accidentally, and it was found that there were two linear fractures on the petrous part of the temporal bone. The question is asking which rule gives the sequence of these fractures.\n\nStudent: What do you mean by the sequence of fractures?\n\nProfessor: The sequence refers to the order in which the fractures occurred. For example, if one fracture happened first and then the other, that would be a different sequence than if the second fracture happened first and then the first fracture.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. So, what are the options again?\n\nProfessor: The options are McNaughton's rule, Puppe's rule, Young's rule, and Dunlop's rule.\n\nStudent: Can you explain each of these rules?\n\nProfessor: McNaughton's rule states that the first fracture is the one closest to the external auditory meatus. Puppe's rule states that the first fracture is the one closest to the foramen lacerum. Young's rule states that the first fracture is the one closest to the internal auditory meatus. Dunlop's rule states that the first fracture is the one closest to the sigmoid sinus.\n\nStudent: Okay, I think I understand. So, which rule applies in this case?\n\nProfessor: Based on the location of the fractures on the petrous part of the temporal bone, Dunlop's rule would apply. The first fracture would be the one closest to the sigmoid sinus.",
