                    {
                        "id": "eab78b52-6939-46fe-a22e-00e80cbc9d69",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Avulsion is the complete displacement of a tooth from its socket due to trauma. It is a dental emergency that requires immediate attention. The success of reimplantation depends on the time elapsed between avulsion and reimplantation, the condition of the tooth, and the management of the avulsed tooth.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the recommended time frame for reimplantation of an avulsed tooth?\n2. What is the proper way to clean the root surface of an avulsed tooth?\n3. When is root canal treatment (RCT) necessary for an avulsed tooth?\n4. Is scrubbing of the root surface a recommended treatment for an avulsed tooth?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The recommended time frame for reimplantation of an avulsed tooth is within 30 minutes of the trauma. After 30 minutes, the success rate of reimplantation decreases significantly.\n2. The proper way to clean the root surface of an avulsed tooth is to gently rinse it with saline solution to remove any debris or dirt. It is important not to scrub or scrape the root surface as this can damage the cells on the surface of the root and reduce the chances of successful reimplantation.\n3. Root canal treatment (RCT) may be necessary for an avulsed tooth if the tooth has been out of the socket for more than 60 minutes, if the tooth has been contaminated with debris or dirt, or if the tooth is immature (has an open apex).\n4. Scrubbing of the root surface is not a recommended treatment for an avulsed tooth as it can damage the cells on the surface of the root and reduce the chances of successful reimplantation.\n\nAnswer to the original question:\nThe recommended treatment for an 8 year old child with an avulsed central incisor that has been contaminated with debris is to clean the root surface with saline and reimplant the tooth (option A). Root canal treatment may be necessary depending on the condition of the tooth, but scrubbing of the root surface is not recommended.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "84983d61-fd9e-4101-a412-35681d0c6b53",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "A) Clean root surface with saline and reimplant.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/30 04:26:21",
