                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: 1-3 beta-d-glucan is a polysaccharide found in the cell walls of many fungi, including Candida, Cryptococcus, Penicillium, and Rhizopus.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the 1-3 beta-d-glucan assay?\n2. How does the assay work?\n3. Which infections are associated with elevated levels of 1-3 beta-d-glucan?\n4. Which of the listed infections are known to cause elevated levels of 1-3 beta-d-glucan?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The 1-3 beta-d-glucan assay is a blood test used to detect the presence of 1-3 beta-d-glucan in the bloodstream.\n2. The assay works by using a substance that binds specifically to 1-3 beta-d-glucan, causing a color change that can be measured and quantified.\n3. Elevated levels of 1-3 beta-d-glucan are associated with fungal infections, particularly those caused by Candida, Aspergillus, and Pneumocystis jirovecii.\n4. Of the listed infections, invasive candidiasis and cryptococcus are known to cause elevated levels of 1-3 beta-d-glucan. Penicillium and rhinocerebral mucormycosis are not typically associated with elevated levels of 1-3 beta-d-glucan. Therefore, the answer is A) Invasive candidiasis.",
