                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Giardia and Entamoeba are parasitic infections that can cause gastrointestinal symptoms. Staph and Clostridium perfringens are bacteria that can cause food poisoning.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the symptoms being experienced?\n- Repeated thin stools with mucus, subjective feeling of fever, lower abdominal pain, and leukocytes in stool.\n2. What are the possible causes of these symptoms?\n- Parasitic infections (Giardia and Entamoeba) or bacterial infections (Staph and Clostridium perfringens).\n3. What are the specific symptoms associated with each possible cause?\n- Giardia and Entamoeba can cause diarrhea, abdominal pain, and fever. Staph and Clostridium perfringens can cause diarrhea, abdominal cramps, and nausea/vomiting.\n4. Are there any additional factors that could help narrow down the possible cause?\n- Presence of leukocytes in stool may suggest an inflammatory process, which is more commonly associated with parasitic infections.\n\nAnswer: Based on the symptoms being experienced and the presence of leukocytes in stool, Giardia or Entamoeba are more likely causes than Staph or Clostridium perfringens. Therefore, the answer is A) Giardia.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "31a82e24-8a69-4989-839b-efd9c784dc6d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
