                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "First, let's understand what calculus is in this context. Calculus, also known as tartar, is a hard, calcified deposit that forms on teeth and contributes to their decay.\n\nNow let's analyze each option:\n\nA) Hydroxyapatite: It is a naturally occurring mineral form of calcium apatite, which is the main component of tooth enamel and bone mineral.\n\nB) Octacalcium phosphate: It is a precursor to hydroxyapatite in the biomineralization process and is found in dental calculus.\n\nC) Magnesium whitlockite: It is a magnesium-containing crystal found in some urinary and salivary stones, but not a main component of dental calculus.\n\nD) Brushite: It is a calcium phosphate mineral that can be found in some urinary stones and occasionally in dental calculus, but not as the main component.\n\nBased on this information, the main crystalline component of calculus is:\n\nA) Hydroxyapatite",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "b344452b-b4e3-437a-845c-d7f88c49e14e",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
