                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nHbA1c is a form of hemoglobin that is used to measure average blood sugar levels over a period of 2-3 months. It is formed when glucose binds to hemoglobin in the blood. \n\nTo measure HbA1c, we need a method that can separate HbA1c from other forms of hemoglobin and quantify it accurately. \n\nA) Isoelectric focusing: This method separates proteins based on their isoelectric point, which is the pH at which they have no net charge. While this method can separate HbA1c from other forms of hemoglobin, it may not be the most accurate method for quantification. \n\nB) Affinity chromatography: This method uses a column with a specific ligand that binds to HbA1c and separates it from other forms of hemoglobin. This method is highly specific and accurate for quantification. \n\nC) Ion exchange chromatography: This method separates proteins based on their charge. While it can separate HbA1c from other forms of hemoglobin, it may not be the most accurate method for quantification. \n\nD) Electrophoresis: This method separates proteins based on their size and charge. While it can separate HbA1c from other forms of hemoglobin, it may not be the most accurate method for quantification. \n\nTherefore, the best method to measure HbA1c is B) Affinity chromatography.",
