                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The nasopharynx is the upper part of the throat behind the nose. Epistaxis is the medical term for nosebleeds. CT scan is a type of imaging test that uses X-rays and computer technology to create detailed images of the body.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the definition of staging in cancer?\n2. What are the different stages of nasopharyngeal cancer?\n3. How is the stage of nasopharyngeal cancer determined?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Staging in cancer refers to the process of determining the extent and spread of the cancer in the body. This information is used to guide treatment decisions and predict the prognosis (outlook) for the patient.\n2. The different stages of nasopharyngeal cancer are:\n- Stage 0: The cancer is only in the lining of the nasopharynx and has not spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes.\n- Stage I: The cancer is in the nasopharynx only and is less than 2 cm in size.\n- Stage II: The cancer has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes and is either less than 4 cm in size or has not spread beyond the nasopharynx.\n- Stage III: The cancer has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes and is larger than 4 cm.\n- Stage IV: The cancer has spread to other parts of the body, such as distant lymph nodes or organs like the lungs or liver.\n3. The stage of nasopharyngeal cancer is determined by a combination of factors, including the size and location of the tumor, whether it has spread to nearby tissues or lymph nodes, and whether it has metastasized (spread) to other parts of the body. Imaging tests like CT scans and MRI scans, as well as biopsies and other diagnostic procedures, are used to gather this information. The stage is then assigned based on a standardized system, such as the TNM staging system. \n\nAnswer to the original question:\nBased on the information provided, the enhancing mass in the nasopharynx with minimal extension into sphenoid sinus and no lateral extension would likely be classified as Stage IIA nasopharyngeal cancer.",
