                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Understand what ALL is and what factors affect prognosis.\n2. Identify the options given and their relevance to ALL prognosis.\n3. Eliminate options that are not poor prognostic indicators.\n4. Choose the option that is a poor prognostic indicator of ALL.\n\nAnswer:\n1. ALL stands for Acute Lymphoblastic Leukemia, which is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow. Prognosis refers to the likely outcome of the disease, including the chances of survival and the response to treatment. Factors that affect prognosis include age, sex, leukocyte count, genetic abnormalities, and response to treatment.\n2. The options given are A) Female sex, B) Leukocyte count < 50,000, C) Age greater than 1 year, and D) Hypodiploidy. Female sex is a demographic factor that may affect prognosis, but it is not a direct indicator of poor prognosis. Leukocyte count is a measure of the number of white blood cells in the blood, which can indicate the severity of the disease. Age is another demographic factor that may affect prognosis, but it is not a direct indicator of poor prognosis. Hypodiploidy is a genetic abnormality that is associated with poor prognosis in ALL.\n3. Option A) Female sex and option C) Age greater than 1 year are not direct indicators of poor prognosis, so they can be eliminated. Option B) Leukocyte count < 50,000 is actually a good prognostic indicator, as it suggests a less severe form of the disease. Option D) Hypodiploidy is a poor prognostic indicator, as it suggests a more aggressive form of the disease.\n4. The correct answer is D) Hypodiploidy.",
