                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Acute lymphoblastic leukemia (ALL) is a type of cancer that affects the blood and bone marrow. It is the most common type of cancer in children, but it can also occur in adults. The prognosis of ALL depends on several factors, including the patient's age, sex, leukocyte count, and genetic abnormalities.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a poor prognostic indicator?\n2. What is hypodiploidy?\n3. How does age affect the prognosis of ALL?\n4. How does leukocyte count affect the prognosis of ALL?\n5. How does sex affect the prognosis of ALL?\n\nAnswer:\n1. A poor prognostic indicator is a factor that suggests a worse outcome for the patient. In the case of ALL, poor prognostic indicators include high leukocyte count, older age, and certain genetic abnormalities.\n2. Hypodiploidy is a genetic abnormality that occurs when a cell has fewer than the normal number of chromosomes. In ALL, hypodiploidy is associated with a worse prognosis.\n3. Age greater than 1 year is a poor prognostic indicator for ALL. Children under the age of 1 have a better prognosis than older children and adults.\n4. Leukocyte count is a significant factor in the prognosis of ALL. Patients with a leukocyte count of less than 50,000 have a better prognosis than those with a higher count.\n5. Sex does not appear to be a significant factor in the prognosis of ALL. Both males and females have similar survival rates. Therefore, option A is not the correct answer. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Hypodiploidy.",
