                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nHepatic first pass metabolism refers to the process by which drugs are metabolized by the liver before they enter the systemic circulation. This can significantly reduce the bioavailability of some drugs, meaning that less of the drug reaches the target site in the body.\n\nThe routes of drug administration that bypass hepatic first pass metabolism include:\n\nB) Intravenous: This route of administration involves injecting the drug directly into a vein, bypassing the liver entirely.\n\nC) Sublingual: This route of administration involves placing the drug under the tongue, where it is absorbed directly into the bloodstream through the sublingual mucosa. This also bypasses the liver.\n\nD) Intradermal: This route of administration involves injecting the drug into the dermis, the layer of skin just below the epidermis. This route does not involve the liver.\n\nTherefore, the answer is A) Oral, as drugs taken orally must pass through the liver before entering the systemic circulation, and are subject to hepatic first pass metabolism.",
                        "answers": [
