                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question\nThe question is asking us to identify the route of drug administration that does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.\n\nStep 2: Understand the concept\nHepatic first pass metabolism refers to the metabolism of a drug by the liver before it enters the systemic circulation. This can significantly reduce the bioavailability of the drug. Some routes of drug administration bypass hepatic first pass metabolism, such as intravenous and sublingual, while others do not, such as oral.\n\nStep 3: Analyze the options\nA) Oral - This route of drug administration does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.\nB) Intravenous - This route of drug administration bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism.\nC) Sublingual - This route of drug administration bypasses hepatic first pass metabolism.\nD) Intradermal - This route of drug administration is not commonly used for systemic drug delivery, but it does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.\n\nStep 4: Identify the correct answer\nThe correct answer is D) Intradermal, as it is not commonly used for systemic drug delivery and does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.\n\nStep 5: Verify the answer\nWe can verify the answer by reviewing the concept of hepatic first pass metabolism and the routes of drug administration that bypass it. Intradermal administration is not commonly used for systemic drug delivery, as it is typically used for local effects, such as in skin testing or allergy shots. Therefore, it does not bypass hepatic first pass metabolism.",
