                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, I have a question about the case you presented. Can you explain what retrograde amnesia is?\n\nProfessor: Of course. Retrograde amnesia is when a person is unable to remember events that occurred before the injury or trauma that caused the amnesia.\n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining that. So, based on the information given, what do you think is the most likely cause of the patient's symptoms?\n\nProfessor: Well, the fact that the patient had a fall and vomited near an alcohol shop suggests that alcohol may have been involved. However, the presence of retrograde amnesia indicates that there may have been some sort of head injury. Based on these factors, I would say that the most likely cause is concussion.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. Can you explain what diffuse axonal injury is and why it's not the most likely cause in this case?\n\nProfessor: Diffuse axonal injury is a type of brain injury that occurs when the brain is shaken or rotated inside the skull. It can cause widespread damage to the brain's white matter. However, in this case, the patient's symptoms are not consistent with diffuse axonal injury. The fact that he was awake with open eyes suggests that he did not suffer a severe brain injury. Additionally, diffuse axonal injury typically causes a prolonged period of unconsciousness, which the patient did not experience.\n\nStudent: I see. And what about cerebral venous thrombosis? Can you explain what that is and why it's not the most likely cause in this case?\n\nProfessor: Cerebral venous thrombosis is a rare condition in which a blood clot forms in the veins that drain blood from the brain. It can cause a variety of symptoms, including headache, seizures, and confusion. However, in this case, the patient's symptoms are not consistent with cerebral venous thrombosis. The fact that he had a fall and vomited near an alcohol shop suggests that alcohol may have been involved, and the presence of retrograde amnesia indicates that there may have been some sort of head injury. Additionally, cerebral venous thrombosis typically causes a gradual onset of symptoms, whereas the patient's symptoms appeared suddenly after the fall. \n\nStudent: Thank you for explaining all of that, professor. It's much clearer to me now why concussion is the most likely cause in this case.",
                        "answers": [
