                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The symptoms of weakness, fatigue, and hypoglycemia suggest that the patient is not able to maintain normal blood glucose levels. Since the patient consumed raw eggs, it is possible that they were contaminated with bacteria that can cause food poisoning. One such bacteria is Salmonella, which can infect the intestinal cells and cause damage to the liver. The liver is responsible for maintaining blood glucose levels by releasing glucose from glycogen stores or by synthesizing glucose from other sources.\n\nThe doctor prescribed vitamin tablets, which suggests that the patient may have a deficiency in a vitamin that is required for normal glucose metabolism. One such vitamin is biotin, which is a cofactor for several enzymes involved in glucose metabolism.\n\nOf the options given, only one enzyme deficiency is associated with hypoglycemia and can be caused by a biotin deficiency. That enzyme is pyruvate carboxylase, which is required for gluconeogenesis, the process by which glucose is synthesized from non-carbohydrate sources. Pyruvate carboxylase requires biotin as a cofactor, and a deficiency in biotin can lead to impaired gluconeogenesis and hypoglycemia.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Pyruvate carboxylase.",
