                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Understand what the Krebs cycle is and how Acetyl-CoA pyruvate is formed.\n2. Identify the amino acids that can form Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n3. Determine which amino acid does not form Acetyl-CoA pyruvate and therefore does not enter the Krebs cycle.\n\nAnswer:\n1. The Krebs cycle is a series of chemical reactions that occur in the mitochondria of cells. It is also known as the citric acid cycle or the tricarboxylic acid cycle. The cycle is responsible for the breakdown of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins into energy in the form of ATP. Acetyl-CoA pyruvate is formed when pyruvate is converted to Acetyl-CoA, which then enters the Krebs cycle.\n2. Amino acids that can form Acetyl-CoA pyruvate include alanine, glycine, and hydroxyproline. Tyrosine, on the other hand, cannot form Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n3. Therefore, the answer is B) Tyrosine.",
                        "answers": [
