                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Among the given amino acids, which one does not participate in the Krebs cycle by producing Acetyl-CoA pyruvate?\n\nStep-by-step solution:\n- The Krebs cycle is a series of chemical reactions that occur in the mitochondria of cells, where Acetyl-CoA is oxidized to produce energy.\n- Acetyl-CoA is formed from the breakdown of glucose, fatty acids, and some amino acids.\n- Pyruvate is a key intermediate in the formation of Acetyl-CoA from glucose, but not from fatty acids or amino acids.\n- Therefore, the amino acid that does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate must be one that does not produce pyruvate as an intermediate.\n- Glycine, Tyrosine, and Alanine can all be converted to pyruvate through different pathways, so they are not the answer.\n- Hydroxyproline, on the other hand, is not known to produce pyruvate as an intermediate, so it is the amino acid that does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate.\n\nAnswer: C) Hydroxyproline.",
