                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The Krebs cycle, also known as the citric acid cycle, is a series of chemical reactions that occur in the mitochondria of cells. It is an important part of cellular respiration, which is the process by which cells convert glucose and oxygen into energy. Acetyl-CoA is a molecule that enters the Krebs cycle and is formed from the breakdown of glucose, fatty acids, and some amino acids.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. Which amino acids can form Acetyl-CoA?\n2. Which amino acids enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate?\n3. Which of the given amino acids can form Acetyl-CoA pyruvate?\n\nAnswering sub-question 1:\nSome amino acids can be converted into Acetyl-CoA through different pathways. These amino acids are called ketogenic amino acids. Examples of ketogenic amino acids are leucine, lysine, phenylalanine, and tyrosine.\n\nAnswering sub-question 2:\nPyruvate is a molecule that can be converted into Acetyl-CoA and enter the Krebs cycle. Some amino acids can form pyruvate through different pathways. These amino acids are called glucogenic amino acids. Examples of glucogenic amino acids are alanine, glycine, and hydroxyproline.\n\nAnswering sub-question 3:\nOut of the given amino acids, glycine, hydroxyproline, and alanine are glucogenic amino acids that can form pyruvate and enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. Tyrosine is a ketogenic amino acid that can form Acetyl-CoA but cannot enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. Therefore, the answer to the original question is B) Tyrosine.",
