                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the Krebs cycle is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the Krebs cycle is a series of chemical reactions that occur in the mitochondria of cells. It's also known as the citric acid cycle or the tricarboxylic acid cycle. The cycle is responsible for producing energy in the form of ATP from the breakdown of carbohydrates, fats, and proteins.\n\nStudent: Okay, so which amino acids enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate?\n\nProfessor: Well, there are three amino acids that can enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate: alanine, leucine, and isoleucine.\n\nStudent: And which of the amino acids listed in the question does not enter the Krebs cycle by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate?\n\nProfessor: The correct answer is B) Tyrosine. Tyrosine is broken down into fumarate and acetoacetate, which can enter the Krebs cycle at different points, but not by forming Acetyl-CoA pyruvate. Glycine, hydroxyproline, and alanine can all be converted into pyruvate, which can then form Acetyl-CoA and enter the Krebs cycle.",
