                    {
                        "id": "94947589-7e97-4b23-8b25-1f1445689cbf",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nThe net filtration pressure (NFP) is calculated as follows:\n\nNFP = (capillary hydrostatic pressure + interstitial oncotic pressure) - (capillary oncotic pressure + interstitial hydrostatic pressure)\n\nWe are given the values of interstitial hydrostatic pressure (2 mm Hg), interstitial oncotic pressure (7 mm Hg), and capillary hydrostatic pressure (25 mm Hg). We need to find the capillary oncotic pressure that would result in a net filtration pressure of 3 mm Hg.\n\nSubstituting the given values and the unknown capillary oncotic pressure (COP) into the NFP equation, we get:\n\n3 = (25 + 7) - (COP + 2)\n\nSimplifying the equation, we get:\n\nCOP = 25 + 7 - 2 - 3 = 27 mm Hg\n\nTherefore, the capillary oncotic pressure that would allow a net filtration pressure of 3 mm Hg is 27 mm Hg. The answer is D) 27.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "cb651b33-9171-4088-9f98-cefae4a7879e",
