                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-17",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what interstitial hydrostatic pressure and interstitial oncotic pressure mean?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Interstitial hydrostatic pressure is the pressure exerted by the fluid in the spaces between cells and tissues. Interstitial oncotic pressure is the pressure exerted by proteins in the interstitial fluid.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand that. What about capillary hydrostatic pressure and capillary oncotic pressure?\n\nProfessor: Capillary hydrostatic pressure is the pressure exerted by the blood in the capillaries. Capillary oncotic pressure is the pressure exerted by proteins in the blood plasma.\n\nStudent: Got it. So, if the net filtration pressure is 3 mm Hg, how do we calculate the capillary oncotic pressure?\n\nProfessor: We can use the formula: Net filtration pressure = (capillary hydrostatic pressure - capillary oncotic pressure) - (interstitial hydrostatic pressure - interstitial oncotic pressure). \n\nStudent: Okay, so we plug in the values we have: 3 = (25 - capillary oncotic pressure) - (2 - 7).\n\nProfessor: Right. Simplifying the equation, we get: 3 = 16 - capillary oncotic pressure. \n\nStudent: So, to solve for capillary oncotic pressure, we subtract 16 from both sides?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. So, we get: -13 = -capillary oncotic pressure. \n\nStudent: Wait, why is the answer negative?\n\nProfessor: That's because the capillary oncotic pressure needs to be lower than the capillary hydrostatic pressure to allow for a net filtration pressure of 3 mm Hg. \n\nStudent: Oh, I see. So, we just need to take the absolute value of -13 to get the answer?\n\nProfessor: No, actually, we need to flip the sign to make it positive. So, the capillary oncotic pressure should be 13 mm Hg. \n\nStudent: Okay, I understand now. So, the answer is not one of the options given?\n\nProfessor: That's correct. The answer is not one of the options given.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ee8cdc49-4242-462b-a3df-f5cc77c932fb",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
