                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in. \n\nIOLs (intraocular lenses) are typically implanted during cataract surgery to replace the natural lens that has become cloudy. In a young male, the IOL may last for many years without any issues. However, as the patient ages and develops presbyopia (difficulty seeing up close), they may require a different type of IOL or additional corrective measures such as reading glasses. In some cases, the IOL may need to be replaced if it becomes damaged or if the patient develops complications such as posterior capsule ossification. Therefore, it is important to monitor the patient's vision and discuss any changes or concerns with their eye doctor.",
