                    {
                        "id": "cba9c19e-b021-40fa-8c88-668827cd2b59",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the scenario\nIn this scenario, an intraocular lens (IOL) has been placed in a young male. The question is what should be done after 10 years.\n\nStep 2: Consider the options\nA) IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification - Posterior capsule ossification is a condition where the capsule behind the IOL becomes cloudy, causing vision problems. In such cases, the IOL may need to be removed. However, this is not the only reason for IOL removal.\nB) Never be removed - This is not a correct answer as IOLs may need to be removed or replaced for various reasons.\nC) Remove when presbyopia sets in - Presbyopia is a condition where the eye loses its ability to focus on near objects. This is a natural part of aging and typically occurs after the age of 40. However, this is not a reason to remove an IOL.\nD) Should be changed after 10 years - This is a possibility as IOLs may need to be replaced due to various reasons such as power change, dislocation, or damage.\n\nStep 3: Choose the best answer\nBased on the options, the best answer is D) Should be changed after 10 years. While IOLs can last for many years, they may need to be replaced due to various reasons such as power change, dislocation, or damage. Therefore, it is recommended to have regular follow-up appointments with an ophthalmologist to monitor the IOL and determine if replacement is necessary.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "8e670bae-e30e-4741-8e1c-eb280b605cde",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
