                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the recommended action for an IOL placed in a young male after 10 years?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Option A suggests that the IOL should be removed if there is posterior capsule ossification. This may be a valid reason for removal, but it is not clear if this is the only reason or if it is a common occurrence after 10 years.\n- Option B suggests that the IOL should never be removed. This seems unlikely, as there may be various reasons for removal or replacement of an IOL over time.\n- Option C suggests that the IOL should be removed when presbyopia sets in. This may be a valid reason for removal, but it is not clear if this is the only reason or if it is a common occurrence after 10 years.\n- Option D suggests that the IOL should be changed after 10 years. This seems like a reasonable option, as IOLs may need to be replaced or updated over time due to various factors such as changes in vision or technology advancements.\n\nCorrect answer: D) Should be changed after 10 years.",
