                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain the options for what should be done with an IOL placed in a young male after 10 years?\n\nProfessor: Sure, I can do that. The first option is that the IOL should be removed in case of Posterior Capsule ossification. This means that if the capsule behind the IOL becomes hard and opaque, the IOL should be removed.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand that. What about the second option?\n\nProfessor: The second option is that the IOL should never be removed. This means that if there are no complications or issues with the IOL, it can stay in place indefinitely.\n\nStudent: I see. And what about the third option?\n\nProfessor: The third option is to remove the IOL when presbyopia sets in. Presbyopia is a condition that affects the eyes as we age, and it makes it harder to focus on close objects. So, if the patient develops presbyopia, the IOL may need to be removed and replaced with a different type of lens.\n\nStudent: Okay, that makes sense. And what about the fourth option?\n\nProfessor: The fourth option is that the IOL should be changed after 10 years. This means that even if there are no complications or issues with the IOL, it may need to be replaced after a certain amount of time has passed.\n\nStudent: Thank you, Professor. I understand the options much better now.",
