                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Lymph nodes are small, bean-shaped structures that are part of the lymphatic system. They are found throughout the body and play an important role in filtering lymph fluid and trapping foreign substances, such as bacteria and viruses. The cortex is the outer layer of the lymph node and contains various structures.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are cords of billroth?\n2. What are Hassall's corpuscles?\n3. What are lymphatic nodules?\n4. What is white pulp?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Cords of billroth are a network of reticular fibers and cells that form a framework within the lymph node cortex. They help to support and organize the lymphoid tissue.\n2. Hassall's corpuscles are structures found in the medulla (inner region) of the thymus gland, not in the cortex of lymph nodes.\n3. Lymphatic nodules are small, spherical clusters of lymphoid tissue found throughout the lymph node cortex. They contain B cells, which produce antibodies to fight infections.\n4. White pulp is a region of the spleen, not the lymph node cortex. It contains lymphocytes and other immune cells that help to filter blood and fight infections.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Lymphatic nodules.",
