                    {
                        "id": "bb475234-8016-4528-ba8b-48b70a0b5f1b",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is B) First pass effect.\n\nThe first pass effect is the process by which the amount of a drug in the body decreases after administration, but before entering the systemic circulation. This occurs because the drug is metabolized by enzymes in the liver before it can enter the bloodstream. As a result, the amount of drug that reaches the systemic circulation is less than the amount that was originally administered.\n\nExcretion refers to the elimination of a drug from the body through urine, feces, or sweat. This occurs after the drug has entered the systemic circulation.\n\nFirst order elimination refers to the process by which the concentration of a drug in the body decreases exponentially over time. This occurs when the drug is eliminated at a constant rate proportional to its concentration in the body.\n\nMetabolism refers to the process by which the body breaks down a drug into smaller molecules that can be eliminated from the body. This can occur in the liver, kidneys, or other organs.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "e9a850ef-3d0f-47e6-b893-ee942c5bd404",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) First pass effect.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/30 01:10:28",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "1468aad2-567a-40d4-ada2-0207ec6432ee",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-13",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the term for the process in which the amount of a drug in the body decreases after administration, but before entering the systemic circulation?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Option A, excretion, refers to the removal of a drug from the body after it has entered the systemic circulation. This does not fit the description in the question.\n- Option D, metabolism, refers to the chemical breakdown of a drug in the body, usually by enzymes in the liver. This can occur both before and after the drug enters the systemic circulation, so it does not specifically describe the process in the question.\n- Option C, first order elimination, refers to the rate at which a drug is eliminated from the body, which follows a first-order kinetic model. This is related to the process in the question, but it does not specifically describe the location of the elimination (before entering the systemic circulation).\n- Option B, first pass effect, refers to the initial metabolism of a drug by the liver before it enters the systemic circulation. This is exactly what the question is asking for, so it is the correct answer.\n\nTherefore, the answer is B) First pass effect.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
