                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: When a drug is administered, it goes through various processes in the body before it reaches the systemic circulation and starts to have its intended effect. One of these processes is the first pass effect, which refers to the metabolism of the drug by the liver before it enters the systemic circulation. This can result in a significant reduction in the amount of the drug that ultimately reaches the target site.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is excretion?\n2. What is first order elimination?\n3. What is metabolism?\n4. How do these processes relate to the original question?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Excretion refers to the removal of a drug or its metabolites from the body, typically through urine or feces.\n2. First order elimination refers to the process by which a constant fraction of a drug is eliminated from the body per unit of time. This is a common way to describe the kinetics of drug elimination.\n3. Metabolism refers to the chemical transformation of a drug or other substance in the body, typically by enzymes in the liver or other organs. This can result in the formation of metabolites that may have different properties than the original drug.\n4. The original question is asking about the process by which the amount of a drug in the body decreases before it enters the systemic circulation. This is specifically referring to the first pass effect, which is a type of metabolism that occurs in the liver. Excretion and first order elimination are related processes that occur after the drug has entered the systemic circulation.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "00b4ddbb-bff5-4e7f-8681-46c23c51b46d",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
