                        "cot": " We know that SCID is a condition where there is a deficiency in immune function. This can be caused by a mutation in any gene involved in immune function. So, we need to look for genes involved in immune function.\n\nThe first gene we will look at is EGFR. EGFR is a gene that encodes for a protein that is involved in cell growth and differentiation. This protein is also involved in the development of the immune system. Mutations in EGFR have been associated with SCID.\n\nThe next gene we will look at is CD23. CD23 is a gene that encodes for a protein that is involved in the development and function of B cells. B cells are a type of white blood cell that is involved in the immune response. Mutations in CD23 have been associated with SCID.\n\nThe next gene we will look at is IL6. IL6 is a gene that encodes for a protein that is involved in the development and function of T cells. T cells are a type of white blood cell that is involved in the immune response. Mutations in IL6 have been associated with SCID.\n\nThe last gene we will look at is IL2RG. IL2RG is a gene that encodes for a protein that is involved in the development and function of T cells. T cells are a type of white blood cell that is involved in the immune response. Mutations in IL2RG have been associated with SCID.",
