                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what X-Linked SCID is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, X-Linked SCID is a severe combined immunodeficiency disorder that is caused by a mutation in the IL2RG gene, which is located on the X chromosome.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what are the genes involved in X-Linked SCID?\n\nProfessor: The genes involved in X-Linked SCID are EGFR, CD23, IL6, and IL2RG.\n\nStudent: How do these genes contribute to X-Linked SCID?\n\nProfessor: Well, EGFR, CD23, and IL6 are not directly involved in X-Linked SCID. However, IL2RG is crucial for the development and function of immune cells, particularly T and NK cells. Mutations in the IL2RG gene prevent the production of functional T and NK cells, leading to the severe immunodeficiency seen in X-Linked SCID.\n\nStudent: How does the mutation in the IL2RG gene cause the immunodeficiency?\n\nProfessor: The IL2RG gene provides instructions for making a protein called the common gamma chain, which is a component of several cytokine receptors, including the receptors for interleukin-2 (IL-2), IL-4, IL-7, IL-9, IL-15, and IL-21. These cytokines are important for the development and function of immune cells. Without a functional common gamma chain, the cytokine receptors cannot signal properly, leading to the impaired development and function of T and NK cells.",
                        "answers": [
