                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "To clarify, the question is asking what the purpose of a reverse bevel incision is. \n\nStep-by-step reasoning: \n\n- A reverse bevel incision is a type of incision made in periodontal surgery. \n- It involves making a cut in the gingiva (gum tissue) at an angle, with the bevel facing towards the tooth. \n- This creates a flap of tissue that can be lifted up to access the underlying structures. \n\nNow, let's consider the answer choices: \n\nA) Allowing atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin: This could be a possible reason for using a reverse bevel incision, as it allows the flap of tissue to be lifted up without damaging the gingival margin. \n\nB) Removing infected tissue in the sulcus: This is not necessarily the main purpose of a reverse bevel incision, although it may be a secondary benefit if the infected tissue is located in the area that is exposed by the flap. \n\nC) Providing access to the alveolar crest: This is another possible reason for using a reverse bevel incision, as it allows the surgeon to access the bone structure underneath the gingiva. \n\nD) All of the above: Based on our reasoning, it seems that A and C are both possible reasons for using a reverse bevel incision, while B is not necessarily the main purpose. Therefore, the correct answer is D. \n\nAnswer: D) All of the above.",
