                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what the reverse bevel incision is?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the reverse bevel incision is a type of incision made in periodontal surgery. It involves making a cut in the gum tissue at an angle, with the bevel facing towards the tooth.\n\nStudent: What is the purpose of making this type of incision?\n\nProfessor: The purpose of the reverse bevel incision is to allow for atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin, which means that the gum tissue can be lifted away from the tooth without causing damage. This is important because it allows the surgeon to access the infected tissue in the sulcus without causing further damage to the surrounding tissue.\n\nStudent: What is the sulcus?\n\nProfessor: The sulcus is the space between the tooth and the gum tissue. In periodontal disease, this space becomes infected with bacteria and other harmful substances, which can lead to inflammation and damage to the surrounding tissue.\n\nStudent: So, does the reverse bevel incision remove the infected tissue in the sulcus?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. The reverse bevel incision is used to remove the infected tissue in the sulcus, which helps to promote healing and prevent further damage to the surrounding tissue.\n\nStudent: Is there any other purpose of the reverse bevel incision?\n\nProfessor: Yes, in addition to allowing for atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin and removing the infected tissue in the sulcus, the reverse bevel incision also provides access to the alveolar crest. The alveolar crest is the bony ridge that supports the teeth, and it can be important to access this area in certain types of periodontal surgery. \n\nStudent: So, the answer to the question is D) All of the above?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's correct. The reverse bevel incision is made to allow for atraumatic reflection of the gingival margin, remove the infected tissue in the sulcus, and provide access to the alveolar crest.",
