                    },
                    {
                        "id": "c5670606-cb9d-4684-8f30-073b2a96495c",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "B) Oxidase is the inflammatory mediator involved in intracellular killing of microbes. \n\nHere's the reasoning: \n\nIntracellular killing of microbes involves the process of phagocytosis, where immune cells engulf and destroy invading pathogens. One of the key mechanisms used by immune cells to kill microbes is the production of reactive oxygen species (ROS) through the action of an enzyme called NADPH oxidase. \n\nROS are highly reactive molecules that can damage microbial membranes and other structures, leading to their destruction. NADPH oxidase is therefore an important mediator of the inflammatory response, and its activity is tightly regulated to prevent damage to host tissues. \n\nOf the answer choices given, only B) Oxidase is directly related to the production of ROS and intracellular killing of microbes. A) Catalase is an enzyme that breaks down hydrogen peroxide, a byproduct of ROS production, but it is not directly involved in intracellular killing. C) Lysozyme is an enzyme that breaks down bacterial cell walls, but it is not specific to intracellular killing. D) IL6 is a cytokine that is involved in the immune response, but it is not directly related to intracellular killing.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "0e4c1793-f0d9-48b2-897c-f1fbeb3f132c",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
