                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: What is the inflammatory mediator involved in intracellular killing of microbes?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Inflammatory mediators are molecules produced by the immune system in response to infection or injury.\n- Intracellular killing of microbes refers to the process by which immune cells destroy pathogens that have invaded their cytoplasm.\n- Catalase, oxidase, and lysozyme are enzymes that can be involved in various aspects of immune defense, but they are not specifically known for intracellular killing of microbes.\n- IL6 is a cytokine that plays a role in inflammation and immune response, but it is not directly involved in intracellular killing of microbes.\n- Therefore, none of the options provided is the correct answer.\n\nAnswer: None of the above.",
                        "answers": [
