                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which structure is most commonly damaged in a 24-year-old college student who injured his right knee while playing hockey and now presents with instability of the knee joint in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion after 3 months?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- The question is asking about the most commonly damaged structure in a specific scenario.\n- The scenario involves a 24-year-old college student who injured his right knee while playing hockey.\n- The student now presents with instability of the knee joint in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion after 3 months.\n- Based on this information, we need to identify the structure that is most commonly damaged in such cases.\n- Option A refers to the posterolateral part of the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL).\n- Option B refers to the anteromedial part of the ACL.\n- Option C refers to the posterior cruciate ligament (PCL).\n- Option D refers to the anterior horn of the medial meniscus.\n- We need to eliminate options based on their relevance to the scenario and the likelihood of being the most commonly damaged structure.\n- The fact that the instability is present in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion suggests that the ACL is involved.\n- The ACL is a commonly injured structure in sports-related knee injuries, especially in young adults.\n- Therefore, the correct answer is A) Posterolateral part of anterior cruciate ligament.\n\nAnswer: A) Posterolateral part of anterior cruciate ligament.",
