                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The knee joint is made up of several structures including ligaments and menisci. The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) and posterior cruciate ligament (PCL) are two important ligaments that provide stability to the knee joint. The medial meniscus is a C-shaped piece of cartilage that sits on the inner side of the knee joint.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. Which ligament is most commonly injured in a knee injury?\n2. What is the function of the ACL and PCL?\n3. What is the function of the medial meniscus?\n4. What is the significance of the patient's symptoms of instability in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion?\n\nAnswers:\n1. The ACL is the most commonly injured ligament in a knee injury.\n2. The ACL and PCL provide stability to the knee joint by preventing excessive forward and backward movement of the tibia (shin bone) in relation to the femur (thigh bone).\n3. The medial meniscus acts as a shock absorber and helps to distribute weight evenly across the knee joint.\n4. The fact that the patient experiences instability in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion suggests that the injury is likely to be to the ACL rather than the PCL, as the ACL is more important for stability in full extension.\n\nAnswer: A) Posterolateral part of anterior cruciate ligament is the structure most commonly damaged in this scenario.",
