                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Hi professor, I have a question about this case. Can you explain what is meant by \"instability of knee joint in its full extension without instability at 90 degrees of flexion\"?\n\nProfessor: Sure, when the knee is fully extended, it should be stable and not move around. However, in this case, the patient is experiencing instability, meaning the knee is moving around more than it should. But when the knee is flexed to 90 degrees, it is stable and not moving around excessively.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand. So, which structure is most commonly damaged in this case?\n\nProfessor: Based on the information given, the most likely structure that is damaged is the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL). And specifically, it is the posterolateral part of the ACL that is commonly injured in cases like this.\n\nStudent: Can you explain why the posterolateral part of the ACL is more commonly injured in this case?\n\nProfessor: Sure, the posterolateral part of the ACL is more vulnerable to injury because it is the part that is under the most stress when the knee is in full extension. This is because the posterolateral part of the ACL is taut when the knee is fully extended, and any sudden movement or twisting can cause it to tear.\n\nStudent: That makes sense. Thank you for explaining it to me, professor.",
