                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Identify the key information in the scenario.\n- 24 years old college student\n- Injured right knee while playing hockey\n- Presents after 3 months\n- Instability of knee joint in full extension\n- No instability at 90 degrees of flexion\n\nStep 2: Understand the anatomy of the knee joint.\n- The knee joint is made up of bones, ligaments, tendons, and cartilage.\n- The ligaments in the knee joint include the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), medial collateral ligament (MCL), and lateral collateral ligament (LCL).\n- The meniscus is a piece of cartilage that acts as a shock absorber between the bones in the knee joint.\n\nStep 3: Determine which structure is most commonly damaged based on the scenario.\n- The fact that the patient has instability in full extension but not at 90 degrees of flexion suggests that the ACL may be damaged.\n- The ACL is commonly injured in sports-related activities, especially those that involve sudden stops or changes in direction.\n- The posterolateral portion of the ACL is the most commonly injured part.\n\nStep 4: Choose the correct answer.\nA) Posterolateral part of anterior cruciate ligament\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Posterolateral part of anterior cruciate ligament.",
