                    {
                        "id": "d99664ce-5bee-4b9d-9b5b-dc4f7d60fab6",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what intractable vertigo means. Intractable vertigo refers to vertigo that is difficult to control or manage with medication or other non-surgical treatments. Meniere's disease is a condition that causes recurrent episodes of vertigo, hearing loss, tinnitus, and a feeling of fullness in the ear.\n\nNow, let's look at the options:\n\nA) Surgical Labyrinthectomy: This surgery involves removing the entire inner ear, including the balance and hearing organs. It is usually reserved for patients with severe and disabling vertigo that cannot be controlled with other treatments. However, it also results in complete hearing loss in the affected ear. Therefore, it is not the best option for Meniere's disease patients who still have some hearing left.\n\nB) Vestibular neurectomy: This surgery involves cutting the vestibular nerve, which carries balance signals from the inner ear to the brain. By cutting this nerve, the brain no longer receives signals from the affected ear, and vertigo is eliminated. However, this surgery also results in complete hearing loss in the affected ear.\n\nC) Endolymphatic sac decompression: This surgery involves opening the endolymphatic sac, which is a small pouch in the inner ear that regulates the balance of fluids. By opening this sac, excess fluid can drain out, reducing the pressure in the inner ear and relieving vertigo. This surgery is less invasive than the previous two options and can preserve some hearing.\n\nD) Cochleosacculotomy: This surgery involves making a small hole in the sacculus, which is a part of the inner ear that is involved in balance and hearing. By making this hole, excess fluid can drain out, reducing the pressure in the inner ear and relieving vertigo. This surgery is also less invasive than the first two options and can preserve some hearing.\n\nTherefore, the best surgery to relieve intractable vertigo in a Meniere's disease patient is either endolymphatic sac decompression or cochleosacculotomy, depending on the individual case and the extent of hearing loss. These surgeries are less invasive than labyrinthectomy or vestibular neurectomy and can preserve some hearing.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "644f08d8-caa4-4cb2-b1ba-a977a222e06a",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "either C) Endolymphatic sac decompression or D) Cochleosacculotomy.",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/11 01:38:52",
