                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nOptic atrophy is a condition where the optic nerve becomes damaged, leading to a loss of vision. It can be caused by a variety of factors, including trauma, inflammation, and certain diseases. \n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa is a genetic disorder that affects the retina, leading to progressive vision loss. While it can cause optic nerve damage, it is not typically associated with optic atrophy. \n\nB) Methanol poisoning can cause optic atrophy, as well as other neurological symptoms. \n\nC) Central retinal aerial occlusion (CRAO) is a blockage of the central retinal artery, which can lead to sudden vision loss. While it does not directly cause optic atrophy, prolonged ischemia (lack of blood flow) to the optic nerve can lead to damage and eventual atrophy. \n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy is a type of macular degeneration that affects the blood vessels in the choroid layer of the eye. It is not typically associated with optic atrophy. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is A) Retinitis pigmentosa.",
