                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nOptic atrophy is a condition where the optic nerve becomes damaged, leading to a loss of vision. It can be caused by a variety of factors, including trauma, infection, and certain medications. Let's look at each option to see if optic atrophy is seen in them:\n\nA) Retinitis pigmentosa: This is a genetic disorder that affects the retina, leading to progressive vision loss. While it can cause optic nerve damage, optic atrophy is not a common feature of this condition.\n\nB) Methanol poisoning: Methanol is a toxic substance that can cause severe damage to the optic nerve, leading to optic atrophy and permanent vision loss.\n\nC) Central retinal aerial occlusion (CRAO): This is a condition where the blood supply to the retina is blocked, leading to sudden vision loss. While it can cause optic nerve damage, optic atrophy is not a common feature of this condition.\n\nD) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy: This is a condition where abnormal blood vessels grow in the choroid, a layer of tissue in the eye. While it can cause vision loss, optic atrophy is not a common feature of this condition.\n\nBased on this analysis, the correct answer is D) Polypoidal choroidal vasculopathy.",
