                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is C) Schedule X.\n\nTo arrive at this answer, we need to understand the different schedules of drugs in India and the regulations surrounding their sale.\n\nIn India, drugs are classified into different schedules based on their potential for abuse and the level of control required to ensure their safe use. The schedules are defined under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940 and the Drugs and Cosmetics Rules, 1945.\n\nSchedule H drugs are prescription drugs that can only be sold by a registered pharmacist on the prescription of a registered medical practitioner. These drugs have a lower potential for abuse and include antibiotics, analgesics, and antipyretics.\n\nSchedule G drugs are over-the-counter drugs that can be sold without a prescription. These drugs have a low potential for abuse and include common painkillers, cough syrups, and antacids.\n\nSchedule X drugs are highly potent and addictive drugs that have a high potential for abuse and dependence. These drugs can only be sold on the prescription of a registered medical practitioner with additional requirements such as a special prescription form, record-keeping, and storage regulations. Examples of Schedule X drugs include opioids, sedatives, and stimulants.\n\nSchedule M drugs are medical devices and diagnostic kits that are regulated under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act. These do not fall under the category of drugs that require a prescription.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Schedule X, as it is the only schedule that requires a prescription from a registered medical practitioner for the sale of drugs.",
