                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: In India, drugs are classified into different schedules based on their potential for abuse and therapeutic value. Schedule H, G, X, and M are different categories of drugs under the Drugs and Cosmetics Act, 1940.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is Schedule H?\n2. What is Schedule G?\n3. What is Schedule X?\n4. What is Schedule M?\n5. Which schedule requires a prescription by a registered medical practitioner?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Schedule H includes drugs that require a prescription and should be sold by a registered pharmacist. These drugs have a lower potential for abuse and are used to treat common ailments such as fever, pain, and infections.\n2. Schedule G includes drugs that are available over-the-counter and do not require a prescription. These drugs have a low potential for abuse and are used to treat minor ailments such as cold, cough, and headache.\n3. Schedule X includes drugs that have a high potential for abuse and addiction. These drugs are strictly regulated and can only be sold on production of a special prescription by a registered medical practitioner. Examples of Schedule X drugs include opioids, sedatives, and stimulants.\n4. Schedule M includes drugs that are used for the manufacture and distribution of other drugs. These drugs are not meant for direct consumption by patients and do not require a prescription.\n5. The drug that must be sold only on production of a prescription by a registered medical practitioner is Schedule X.",
