                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain which hormone is predominantly released by fetal adrenals?\n\nProfessor: Sure, fetal adrenals predominantly release one hormone. Do you know what that hormone is?\n\nStudent: No, I don't. Can you tell me?\n\nProfessor: Of course. The hormone predominantly released by fetal adrenals is testosterone.\n\nStudent: How do fetal adrenals release testosterone?\n\nProfessor: Fetal adrenals release testosterone in response to signals from the pituitary gland. The pituitary gland secretes adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH), which stimulates the fetal adrenal gland to produce testosterone.\n\nStudent: What is the function of testosterone in fetal development?\n\nProfessor: Testosterone plays a crucial role in the development of male reproductive organs, such as the penis and testes. It also contributes to the development of secondary sexual characteristics, such as facial hair and a deep voice, during puberty.\n\nStudent: Is testosterone the only hormone released by fetal adrenals?\n\nProfessor: No, fetal adrenals also release other hormones such as aldosterone and cortisol, but testosterone is the predominant hormone.",
