                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: \n- Coarse facial features, macroglossia, and thick lips are characteristic features of Hurler's syndrome.\n- Copious mucous discharge from the nose can be a symptom of Hurler's syndrome.\n- Enlarged liver and spleen are common in Hurler's syndrome.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is Hurler's syndrome?\n2. What are the symptoms of Hurler's syndrome?\n3. How is Hurler's syndrome diagnosed?\n4. What are the treatment options for Hurler's syndrome?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Hurler's syndrome is a rare genetic disorder that affects the body's ability to break down certain sugars. It is caused by a deficiency in the enzyme alpha-L-iduronidase, which leads to the accumulation of certain substances in the body's cells and tissues.\n2. Symptoms of Hurler's syndrome include coarse facial features, macroglossia (enlarged tongue), thick lips, clouding of the cornea, hearing loss, joint stiffness, and developmental delays.\n3. Hurler's syndrome can be diagnosed through a combination of physical examination, medical history, and laboratory tests. These may include blood and urine tests, genetic testing, and imaging studies such as X-rays or MRI.\n4. Treatment for Hurler's syndrome typically involves enzyme replacement therapy, which involves regular infusions of the missing enzyme. Other treatments may include surgery to correct physical abnormalities, such as cataracts or joint problems, and supportive care to manage symptoms and improve quality of life. Without treatment, Hurler's syndrome can lead to severe developmental delays, organ damage, and early death. \n\nBased on the information provided, the correct diagnosis is A) Hurler's syndrome.",
