                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The correct answer is D) Scleroderma.\n\nTo arrive at this answer, we need to understand what anti-centromere antibodies are and which conditions they are associated with.\n\nAnti-centromere antibodies are autoantibodies that target the centromere, a region of the chromosome that plays a critical role in cell division. These antibodies are commonly found in patients with scleroderma, a connective tissue disorder characterized by fibrosis and vascular abnormalities.\n\nOption A) Drug-induced lupus is a type of lupus that is caused by certain medications. While lupus is associated with a variety of autoantibodies, anti-centromere antibodies are not typically seen in this condition.\n\nOption B) SLE, or systemic lupus erythematosus, is a chronic autoimmune disease that can affect multiple organs and tissues. While SLE is associated with a variety of autoantibodies, anti-centromere antibodies are not typically seen in this condition.\n\nOption C) Sjogren syndrome is an autoimmune disorder that primarily affects the salivary and lacrimal glands, leading to dry mouth and dry eyes. While Sjogren syndrome is associated with a variety of autoantibodies, anti-centromere antibodies are not typically seen in this condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Scleroderma.",
