                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Anti-centromere antibodies are autoantibodies that target the centromere, a region of the chromosome that plays a role in cell division. These antibodies are associated with certain autoimmune diseases.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are the characteristics of drug-induced lupus?\n2. What is SLE?\n3. What is Sjogren syndrome?\n4. What is scleroderma?\n5. Which autoimmune diseases are associated with anti-centromere antibodies?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Drug-induced lupus is a condition in which certain medications can cause symptoms similar to those of systemic lupus erythematosus (SLE), such as joint pain, fever, and rash. However, drug-induced lupus typically resolves once the medication is stopped.\n2. SLE is a chronic autoimmune disease that can affect multiple organs and tissues, including the skin, joints, kidneys, and heart. Symptoms can include fatigue, joint pain, skin rashes, and fever.\n3. Sjogren syndrome is an autoimmune disease that primarily affects the salivary and lacrimal glands, leading to dry mouth and dry eyes. Other symptoms can include joint pain, fatigue, and skin rashes.\n4. Scleroderma is a group of autoimmune diseases that can cause thickening and hardening of the skin and connective tissues. It can also affect internal organs such as the lungs, heart, and kidneys.\n5. Anti-centromere antibodies are most commonly associated with scleroderma, particularly the limited cutaneous form of the disease. They are present in up to 60-80% of patients with this form of scleroderma. They may also be seen in a smaller percentage of patients with other autoimmune diseases, such as Sjogren syndrome and SLE, but are not typically a defining feature of these conditions. Therefore, the correct answer is D) Scleroderma.",
