                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Define SIADH and Pressor Escape.\n2. Understand the mechanism of SIADH.\n3. Understand the mechanism of Pressor Escape.\n4. Compare the characteristics of Pressor Escape with the given options.\n5. Choose the option that rightly describes the mechanism of Pressor Escape in SIADH.\n\nAnswer:\nSIADH (Syndrome of Inappropriate Antidiuretic Hormone) is a condition characterized by excessive secretion of antidiuretic hormone (ADH) from the pituitary gland, leading to water retention and dilutional hyponatremia. Pressor Escape is a compensatory mechanism that occurs in response to chronic hyponatremia, where the body increases the secretion of natriuretic peptides, leading to increased sodium excretion and escape from the pressor effects of ADH.\n\nThe mechanism of SIADH involves the excessive secretion of ADH, which leads to increased water reabsorption in the kidneys, resulting in dilutional hyponatremia. On the other hand, the mechanism of Pressor Escape involves the compensatory secretion of natriuretic peptides, which leads to increased sodium excretion and escape from the pressor effects of ADH.\n\nComparing the given options, we can see that option D is the correct answer as it describes the mechanism of Pressor Escape as sudden decrease in urine volume with increase in urine osmolality dependent of circulating vasopressin levels.",
