                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Autoinfection refers to the process by which an individual becomes infected with a parasite by their own fecal-oral route. In other words, the parasite is able to complete its life cycle within the same host. \n\nTo answer this question, we need to determine which of the listed helminths is not capable of causing autoinfection. \n\nA) Enterobius vermicularis, also known as pinworm, is a common cause of autoinfection. The adult female worm migrates to the perianal area to lay eggs, which can then be ingested by the same host, leading to reinfection. \n\nB) Hymenolepis nana, also known as dwarf tapeworm, is another helminth that can cause autoinfection. The eggs are ingested and hatch in the small intestine, where they develop into adult worms that can lay eggs and perpetuate the infection. \n\nC) Taenia solium, also known as pork tapeworm, can cause autoinfection in rare cases. The adult worm can produce self-fertilized eggs, which can then hatch and develop into larvae that can migrate to other tissues in the same host. \n\nD) Ascaris lumbricoides, also known as roundworm, is the only helminth listed that is not capable of causing autoinfection. The adult worms live in the small intestine and release eggs that are passed in the feces. The eggs must be ingested by a new host to continue the life cycle. \n\nTherefore, the correct answer is D) Ascaris lumbricoides.",
