                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which of the following helminths does not cause autoinfection?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- Autoinfection refers to the process by which a person becomes reinfected with a parasite from their own body, rather than from an external source.\n- Enterobius vermicularis, also known as pinworm, is known to cause autoinfection. This is because the female pinworm lays eggs in the perianal area, which can then be ingested by the same person or others in close contact.\n- Hymenolepis nana, also known as dwarf tapeworm, can also cause autoinfection. This is because the eggs of this tapeworm can hatch within the intestine and infect the same person again.\n- Taenia solium, also known as pork tapeworm, can cause autoinfection if the larval form of the parasite (cysticercus) is ingested and develops into an adult tapeworm within the intestine.\n- Ascaris lumbricoides, also known as roundworm, can also cause autoinfection. This is because the eggs of this parasite can hatch within the intestine and infect the same person again.\n\n- Therefore, the answer is B) Hymenolepis nana, as it is the only helminth listed that does not cause autoinfection.\n\nAnswer: B) Hymenolepis nana",
