                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what autoinfection is?\n\nProfessor: Autoinfection is when a person becomes infected with a parasite from their own body, rather than from an external source.\n\nStudent: Okay, so what are helminths?\n\nProfessor: Helminths are parasitic worms that can infect humans.\n\nStudent: Got it. So which of the helminths listed can't cause autoinfection?\n\nProfessor: The answer is C) Taenia solium. This is because Taenia solium is a tapeworm that requires an intermediate host, such as a pig, to complete its life cycle. It cannot complete its life cycle in humans alone, so autoinfection is not possible.\n\nStudent: And what about the other three options?\n\nProfessor: Enterobius vermicularis, Hymenolepis nana, and Ascaris lumbricoides can all cause autoinfection. Enterobius vermicularis, also known as pinworm, can lay eggs around the anus, which can be ingested and cause reinfection. Hymenolepis nana, a dwarf tapeworm, can complete its life cycle in humans alone and can cause autoinfection. Ascaris lumbricoides, a roundworm, can also cause autoinfection by releasing eggs that can be ingested and cause reinfection.",
