                        "cot": "The translocation t(ll;22) is a characteristic genetic abnormality found in Ewing's sarcoma. To detect this translocation in the biopsy sample from the eight-year-old child with leg swelling, the best method would be:\n\nC) FISH (Fluorescence In Situ Hybridization)\n\nFISH is a molecular cytogenetic technique that uses fluorescent probes to bind to specific DNA sequences and visualize the location of genes or chromosomal abnormalities. It is a highly sensitive and specific method for detecting translocations, deletions, duplications, and other chromosomal abnormalities in cancer cells. FISH can be performed on interphase nuclei, which allows for rapid detection of chromosomal abnormalities without the need for cell culture or metaphase spreads. In contrast, conventional karyotyping requires cell culture and metaphase spreads, which can take several days to weeks to obtain results. Next-generation sequencing and PCR are not typically used for detecting translocations in Ewing's sarcoma.",
