                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Ewing's sarcoma is a rare type of bone cancer that usually affects children and young adults. It is caused by a translocation between chromosomes 11 and 22, resulting in the fusion of the EWSR1 and FLI1 genes. This fusion gene is present in more than 90% of Ewing's sarcoma cases and is considered a hallmark of the disease.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is translocation t(ll;22)?\n2. Why is it important to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma?\n3. What are the different methods to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma?\n4. Which method is the best to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma?\n\n1. Translocation t(ll;22) is a genetic abnormality that occurs when a piece of chromosome 11 breaks off and attaches to chromosome 22, or vice versa. This results in the fusion of the EWSR1 and FLI1 genes, which produces a chimeric protein that drives the development of Ewing's sarcoma.\n2. Detecting translocation t(ll;22) is important for the diagnosis and management of Ewing's sarcoma. It is a specific genetic abnormality that is present in the majority of cases, and its detection can confirm the diagnosis and guide treatment decisions.\n3. The different methods to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma include conventional karyotyping, fluorescence in situ hybridization (FISH), polymerase chain reaction (PCR), and next generation sequencing (NGS).\n4. FISH is the best method to detect translocation t(ll;22) in Ewing's sarcoma. It is a highly sensitive and specific technique that can detect the fusion gene in interphase nuclei and formalin-fixed paraffin-embedded tissue samples. Conventional karyotyping is less sensitive and requires live cells, while PCR and NGS are more complex and expensive methods that may not be readily available in all laboratories.",
