                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the condition\nTetralogy of Fallot is a congenital heart defect that involves four abnormalities in the heart's structure. These abnormalities include a ventricular septal defect (a hole between the two lower chambers of the heart), pulmonary stenosis (narrowing of the pulmonary valve and artery), an overriding aorta (the aorta is shifted to the right and sits over the ventricular septal defect), and right ventricular hypertrophy (thickening of the right ventricle due to increased workload).\n\nStep 2: Consider the effects of the condition\nThe combination of these abnormalities can cause reduced blood flow to the lungs and decreased oxygen levels in the body. This can lead to symptoms such as cyanosis (bluish discoloration of the skin and mucous membranes), shortness of breath, and fatigue.\n\nStep 3: Determine the best position for the child\nTo improve blood flow to the lungs and increase oxygen levels in the body, the child with tetralogy of Fallot should assume a position that reduces the workload on the heart and improves blood flow. The best position for this is squatting, which increases systemic vascular resistance and decreases the amount of blood that flows through the ventricular septal defect. This helps to maintain adequate oxygen levels in the body and reduce symptoms of cyanosis and shortness of breath.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Squatting.",
