                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "it is important to understand what tetralogy of Fallot is. It is a congenital heart defect that involves four abnormalities in the heart's structure, including a ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta. These abnormalities can cause decreased oxygen levels in the blood.\n\nTo improve oxygenation, a child with tetralogy of Fallot may use the squatting position. This position increases systemic vascular resistance, which helps to redirect blood flow to the lungs and improve oxygenation. Leaning forwards may also be helpful in improving oxygenation by reducing the workload on the heart and improving blood flow to the lungs. Supine and prone positions are not typically recommended for children with tetralogy of Fallot as they can worsen symptoms and decrease oxygenation.",
