                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What position should a child with tetralogy of fallot use?\nTo answer this question, we need to understand what tetralogy of fallot is and how it affects the body. Tetralogy of fallot is a congenital heart defect that affects the flow of blood through the heart and to the lungs. It consists of four abnormalities: a ventricular septal defect, pulmonary stenosis, right ventricular hypertrophy, and an overriding aorta. These abnormalities can cause low oxygen levels in the blood and lead to cyanosis (a bluish tint to the skin). \n\nNow, let's consider the options:\nA) Supine - lying on the back. This position may worsen cyanosis in children with tetralogy of fallot because it increases blood flow to the lungs, which can exacerbate the pulmonary stenosis.\nB) Prone - lying on the stomach. This position may not be comfortable for children with tetralogy of fallot and may not have any significant effect on their condition.\nC) Squatting - bending the knees and lowering the body. This position can help improve blood flow to the lungs and increase oxygen levels in the blood, making it a recommended position for children with tetralogy of fallot.\nD) Leaning forwards - bending forward at the waist. This position can also help improve blood flow to the lungs and increase oxygen levels in the blood, making it another recommended position for children with tetralogy of fallot.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is C) Squatting and D) Leaning forwards.",
