                    },
                    {
                        "id": "08a37168-d773-4905-9b14-5168b74fcd53",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, let's understand what a positive pivot shift test is. It is a clinical test used to diagnose instability in the knee joint. During the test, the examiner applies a valgus force (pushing the knee inward) while simultaneously rotating the tibia (lower leg bone) externally. If the tibia subluxates (shifts) anteriorly on the femur (thigh bone) during this maneuver, it indicates a positive pivot shift test.\n\nNow, let's consider the options:\n\nA) Posterior cruciate ligament (PCL): Injury to the PCL would not cause a positive pivot shift test. Instead, it would cause posterior sag of the tibia on the femur, which can be tested using the posterior drawer test.\n\nB) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL): Injury to the ACL is the most common cause of a positive pivot shift test. The ACL is responsible for preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur, and its rupture can lead to instability and subluxation during the pivot shift test.\n\nC) Medial meniscus: Injury to the medial meniscus can cause pain and clicking during the pivot shift test, but it would not cause a positive result. The pivot shift test is primarily used to diagnose ligamentous instability.\n\nD) Lateral meniscus: Similar to the medial meniscus, injury to the lateral meniscus can cause pain and clicking during the pivot shift test, but it would not cause a positive result.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL).",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "77a8b88c-6470-4897-81c4-30cd23a1d7f7",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Anterior cruciate ligament (ACL).",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/03/13 18:54:36",
                        "api_service": "openai_chat",
                        "model": "{'name': 'gpt-3.5-turbo', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
                        "annotations": []
                    },
                    {
                        "id": "799c19c5-9627-42ca-85c5-018844a834f9",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "kojima-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": " The question is asking about a \"positive pivot shift test in knee.\" This likely refers to a physical examination maneuver performed by a healthcare provider. The test is positive if, during the maneuver, the knee joint shifts out of place. This shift is likely due to an injury. \n\nThere are four main ligaments that support the knee joint: the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), the posterior cruciate ligament (PCL), the medial collateral ligament (MCL), and the lateral collateral ligament (LCL). Of these, the ACL and PCL are the most important in stabilizing the knee joint.\n\nThe ACL is located in the front of the knee, and the PCL is located in the back. These ligaments cross each other in the middle of the knee joint. The ACL attaches the femur (thigh bone) to the tibia (shin bone), and the PCL attaches the femur to the tibia.\n\nThe MCL is located on the inside of the knee, and the LCL is located on the outside. These ligaments attach the femur to the tibia.\n\nInjuries to the ACL and PCL are the most common cause of a positive pivot shift test. These ligaments can be torn by a sudden twisting motion of the knee, such as might occur during a football tackle.\n\nThe MCL and LCL can also be torn, but these ligaments are less likely to cause a positive pivot shift test.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "ceab8f95-b7b8-47f3-a8b3-27f652e332f0",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": " B, Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                                "correct_answer": true
                            }
                        ],
                        "author": "thoughtsource",
                        "date": "2023/02/27 14:14:55",
                        "api_service": "openai",
                        "model": "{'name': 'text-davinci-002', 'temperature': 0, 'max_tokens': 512}",
                        "comment": "",
