                    {
                        "id": "c6cd62ac-8ec5-401a-91f5-599b57ab342d",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-10",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Understand what a positive pivot shift test is.\n2. Identify the structures involved in the pivot shift test.\n3. Understand the function of each structure.\n4. Determine which structure is injured based on the positive pivot shift test.\n\nAnswer:\nA positive pivot shift test is a clinical test used to diagnose knee instability. It is performed by flexing the knee to 90 degrees and applying a valgus force while internally rotating the tibia. A positive test is indicated by a sudden shift or clunk as the tibia reduces back into place. \n\nThe structures involved in the pivot shift test are the anterior cruciate ligament (ACL), lateral collateral ligament (LCL), lateral meniscus, and medial meniscus. \n\nThe ACL is responsible for preventing anterior translation of the tibia on the femur and rotational stability. The LCL provides lateral stability to the knee joint. The lateral and medial menisci are responsible for shock absorption and load distribution within the knee joint. \n\nBased on the function of each structure, a positive pivot shift test is most commonly associated with an injury to the ACL. Therefore, the correct answer is B) Anterior cruciate ligament.",
                        "answers": [
