                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nAntigen-presenting cells (APCs) are cells that present antigens to T cells in order to activate an immune response. \n\nA) M-cells are specialized cells found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) that transport antigens from the lumen of the gut to underlying immune cells. M-cells are considered a type of APC, so this option is not the correct answer. \n\nB) Macrophages are a type of immune cell that engulf and digest foreign substances, including pathogens and dead cells. They are also considered a type of APC, so this option is not the correct answer. \n\nC) Langerhans cells are a type of dendritic cell found in the skin and mucous membranes. They are specialized in presenting antigens to T cells and are considered a type of APC, so this option is not the correct answer. \n\nD) Thymocytes are immature T cells that are still developing in the thymus gland. They are not considered APCs because they do not present antigens to other immune cells. Therefore, the correct answer is D) Thymocytes.",
                        "answers": [
