                        "cot": "Rephrased question: Which of the following cells is not an antigen-presenting cell?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n- Antigen-presenting cells are cells that present antigens to T cells to activate an immune response.\n- Option A, M-cells, are specialized cells in the gut-associated lymphoid tissue that transport antigens from the gut lumen to immune cells. Therefore, they are antigen-presenting cells.\n- Option B, macrophages, are phagocytic cells that engulf and digest pathogens, and then present the antigens to T cells. Therefore, they are antigen-presenting cells.\n- Option C, Langerhans cells, are specialized dendritic cells in the skin that capture antigens and migrate to lymph nodes to present them to T cells. Therefore, they are antigen-presenting cells.\n- Option D, thymocytes, are immature T cells that develop in the thymus. They do not present antigens to other T cells, so they are not antigen-presenting cells.\n\nAnswer: D) Thymocytes.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "cceab34c-59f6-4542-8db4-7308de135952",
