                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Antigen-presenting cells (APCs) are cells that display antigens on their surface to activate T cells. M-cells are specialized cells found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) that transport antigens from the lumen of the gut to underlying immune cells. Macrophages are phagocytic cells that engulf and digest foreign substances, including pathogens and dead cells. Langerhans cells are specialized dendritic cells found in the skin and mucous membranes that capture and present antigens to T cells. Thymocytes are immature T cells that develop in the thymus gland.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What are antigen-presenting cells?\n2. What is the function of M-cells?\n3. What is the function of macrophages?\n4. What are Langerhans cells?\n5. What are thymocytes?\n6. Which of the given options is not an antigen-presenting cell?\n\nAnswers:\n1. Antigen-presenting cells are cells that display antigens on their surface to activate T cells.\n2. M-cells are specialized cells found in the mucosa-associated lymphoid tissue (MALT) that transport antigens from the lumen of the gut to underlying immune cells.\n3. Macrophages are phagocytic cells that engulf and digest foreign substances, including pathogens and dead cells.\n4. Langerhans cells are specialized dendritic cells found in the skin and mucous membranes that capture and present antigens to T cells.\n5. Thymocytes are immature T cells that develop in the thymus gland.\n6. The answer is D) Thymocytes, as they are not antigen-presenting cells.",
                        "answers": [
