                    {
                        "id": "67e65eea-7a10-441d-b5fb-acb0a979d018",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": null,
                        "cot_trigger": "zhou-01",
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Step 1: Identify the research question\nThe research question is to compare the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants as compared to term infants.\n\nStep 2: Determine the type of study design needed\nSince the research question involves comparing two groups (pre-term infants and term infants) and their breast milk fat content, we need a study design that allows for comparison between groups.\n\nA) Case control: This study design involves comparing individuals with a specific condition (cases) to individuals without the condition (controls). It is not suitable for our research question, as we are not comparing individuals with and without a specific condition, but rather comparing two groups based on their birth status (pre-term vs term).\n\nB) Prospective cohort: This study design involves following a group of individuals over time and comparing the outcomes of interest between different groups within the cohort. This could be a suitable study design for our research question, as we could follow a cohort of mothers with pre-term and term infants and compare the fat content in their breast milk.\n\nC) Longitudinal study: This study design involves collecting data from the same individuals over time. While this could potentially be used to answer our research question, it is not as well-suited as a prospective cohort study, as it does not specifically involve comparing different groups within the study population.\n\nD) Ambispective: This study design involves a combination of retrospective (looking back at past data) and prospective (collecting new data) components. While this could potentially be used to answer our research question, it is not as well-suited as a prospective cohort study, as it does not specifically involve comparing different groups within the study population.\n\nBased on this analysis, the best-suited study design for the research question is:\n\nB) Prospective cohort",
                        "answers": [
                            {
                                "id": "835f5e1d-2df3-448c-84d2-fca997bc5a20",
                                "answer_extraction": "kojima-A-D",
                                "answer_extraction_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}{cot}\n{answer_extraction}",
                                "answer_extraction_text": "",
                                "answer": "B) Prospective cohort",
                                "correct_answer": false
                            }
