                    {
                        "id": "ff854a75-d1f2-496f-be90-bcfc7535dbe7",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-10",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Plan:\n1. Define each study design to understand their characteristics and suitability for the research question.\n2. Evaluate each study design based on the research question and choose the best-suited design.\n\n1. Study designs:\na) Case-control study: This study design compares individuals with a specific outcome (cases) to those without the outcome (controls) to identify potential risk factors. It is retrospective and relies on recall or medical records.\nb) Prospective cohort study: This study design follows a group of individuals over time to identify potential risk factors and outcomes. It is forward-looking and collects data through surveys, medical records, and physical exams.\nc) Longitudinal study: This study design follows a group of individuals over an extended period to identify changes in outcomes and potential risk factors. It is forward-looking and collects data through surveys, medical records, and physical exams.\nd) Ambispective study: This study design combines retrospective and prospective data collection to identify potential risk factors and outcomes.\n\n2. Evaluation:\nThe research question aims to compare the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants to term infants. Therefore, a study design that follows a group of individuals over time and collects data through surveys, medical records, and physical exams is best suited. Both prospective cohort and longitudinal study designs fit this criteria. However, a longitudinal study design may be more appropriate as it follows individuals over an extended period, allowing for the identification of changes in outcomes and potential risk factors. Therefore, the best-suited study design for this research question is C) Longitudinal study.",
