                    {
                        "id": "87c51caa-a769-4a16-bb27-ca877d7e3507",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-12",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "The best study design for comparing the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants to term infants would be a longitudinal study. \n\nA case control study would not be appropriate because it is designed to compare individuals with a certain condition to those without the condition, and in this case, all infants have the condition of being born. \n\nA prospective cohort study would not be ideal because it would require following a large group of infants over time, which could be difficult and expensive. \n\nAn ambispective study is not a recognized study design and is not appropriate for this research question. \n\nA longitudinal study, on the other hand, would involve following a group of pre-term and term infants over time and collecting data on their breast milk fat content at various intervals. This would allow for a direct comparison between the two groups and would provide valuable information on the differences in breast milk composition between pre-term and term infants.",
                        "answers": [
                            {
