                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "What is the most appropriate study design for comparing the fat content in expressed breast milk of pre-term infants to term infants?\n\nStep-by-step reasoning:\n\n- The study aims to compare the fat content in expressed breast milk of two groups: pre-term and term infants.\n- To compare two groups, we need a study design that allows for the comparison of outcomes between groups.\n- Case-control studies are not appropriate for this question because they are retrospective and compare individuals with a certain outcome to those without the outcome. We want to compare two groups with different outcomes (pre-term vs. term infants).\n- Ambispective is not a recognized study design and is not appropriate for this question.\n- Longitudinal studies follow a group of individuals over time and can be used to compare outcomes between groups. However, they can be time-consuming and expensive.\n- Prospective cohort studies are the most appropriate study design for this question because they follow a group of individuals with a certain exposure (pre-term or term birth) and compare outcomes (fat content in expressed breast milk) between the groups.\n\nTherefore, the best-suited study design for comparing the fat content in expressed breast milk of pre-term infants to term infants is B) Prospective cohort.",
