                    {
                        "id": "a1dfafd8-83a5-4487-b439-f3b48b578ed4",
                        "fragments_version": "0.01",
                        "instruction": "qa-16",
                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: Pre-term infants are born before 37 weeks of gestation and have different nutritional needs than term infants. Breast milk is the recommended source of nutrition for both pre-term and term infants.\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is a case control study?\n2. What is a prospective cohort study?\n3. What is a longitudinal study?\n4. What is an ambispective study?\n5. Which study design is best suited for comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants?\n\nAnswers:\n1. A case control study is a type of observational study where individuals with a certain condition (cases) are compared to individuals without the condition (controls) to identify potential risk factors.\n2. A prospective cohort study is a type of observational study where a group of individuals with a certain characteristic or exposure are followed over time to identify potential outcomes.\n3. A longitudinal study is a type of observational study where a group of individuals are followed over a long period of time to identify changes or patterns in their health or behavior.\n4. An ambispective study is a type of study that combines retrospective and prospective data collection methods.\n5. The best study design for comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants would be a prospective cohort study, where a group of pre-term and term infants are followed over time to measure the fat content in their breast milk. This study design would allow for the comparison of fat content between the two groups and would also allow for the identification of potential factors that may affect the fat content in breast milk.",
                        "answers": [
