                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain what each study design means?\n\nProfessor: Sure. A case control study is when you compare a group of people with a certain condition to a group without the condition. A prospective cohort study is when you follow a group of people over time and observe their outcomes. A longitudinal study is when you follow a group of people over a long period of time and observe changes in their health. And ambispective is not a real study design, so we can eliminate that option.\n\nStudent: Okay, so which one would be best for comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants?\n\nProfessor: Based on the question, we want to compare the fat content in breast milk of two different groups of infants. So, we can eliminate the case control study design. A longitudinal study would be too long and expensive for this type of research. Therefore, the best option would be a prospective cohort study, where we can follow both groups of infants over time and observe their outcomes, including the fat content in their breast milk.",
