                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nStep 1: Understand the question and the variables involved.\nThe question is asking about a study that compares the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term infants and term infants. The variables involved are the type of infant (pre-term or term) and the fat content in the expressed breast milk.\n\nStep 2: Consider the study designs and their characteristics.\nA) Case control: This study design compares individuals with a certain condition (cases) to individuals without the condition (controls) to determine the cause or risk factors for the condition. This design is not suitable for comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants because it does not involve following individuals over time and measuring the outcome of interest.\nB) Prospective cohort: This study design involves following a group of individuals over time and measuring the exposure and outcome of interest. This design could be suitable for comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants if a group of pre-term and term infants are followed over time and their breast milk is measured for fat content.\nC) Longitudinal study: This study design involves following a group of individuals over time and measuring the outcome of interest. This design could be suitable for comparing the fat content in breast milk of pre-term and term infants if a group of pre-term and term infants are followed over time and their breast milk is measured for fat content.\nD) Ambispective: This is not a commonly used study design and it is unclear what it entails.\n\nStep 3: Choose the best study design.\nBased on the characteristics of the study designs and the variables involved, the best study design for comparing the fat content in the expressed breast milk of pre-term and term infants is a longitudinal study. This design involves following a group of individuals over time and measuring the outcome of interest, which is the fat content 