                        "cot": "it is important to understand the basics of genetics and how eye color is inherited. Eye color is determined by multiple genes, but the most important one is called OCA2. This gene comes in two versions, one that produces brown eyes and one that produces blue, green, or gray eyes. The brown version is dominant, meaning that if a person inherits one copy of the brown version and one copy of the blue/green/gray version, they will have brown eyes. However, if a person inherits two copies of the blue/green/gray version, they will have blue, green, or gray eyes.\n\nWith this knowledge, the best way to guess a baby's eye color is to look at the eye colors of their parents and grandparents. If both parents have brown eyes, but one or both of their parents (the baby's grandparents) have blue or green eyes, there is a chance that the baby could inherit the blue/green/gray version of the OCA2 gene and have blue, green, or gray eyes. However, if both parents and all grandparents have brown eyes, it is very likely that the baby will also have brown eyes.\n\nIt is important to note that eye color can be influenced by other factors such as lighting, clothing, and even mood, so it is not always easy to predict with certainty. Additionally, eye color can change during the first year of life as the baby's eyes adjust to light and develop more pigment, so even if a baby is born with blue eyes, they may eventually turn brown.",
