                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, I have a question about the balloons that were left in the living room after the party. What happens to the balloons when they are exposed to the heat from the fireplace?\n\nProfessor: That's a great question, let's break it down. What do you think will happen to the balloons?\n\nStudent: Hmm, I'm not sure. Will they pop?\n\nProfessor: That's a possibility, but let's think about the properties of the balloons. What gas is inside the balloons?\n\nStudent: Oh, right. They were filled with helium.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. And what do we know about helium?\n\nStudent: It's a gas that is lighter than air.\n\nProfessor: Correct. So, if the balloons are exposed to heat, what do you think will happen to the helium gas inside?\n\nStudent: Will it expand?\n\nProfessor: Yes, that's right. When gases are heated, they expand. So, the balloons will expand as well.\n\nStudent: Okay, I understand that part. But what about the fireplace? Will it have any effect on the balloons?\n\nProfessor: Good question. The fireplace will heat up the air in the room, which will cause the balloons to expand even more. However, if the balloons get too close to the fireplace, they could pop.\n\nStudent: Oh, I see. So, the balloons will expand because of the heat from the fireplace and the helium gas inside, but they could also pop if they get too close to the fire.\n\nProfessor: Exactly. It's important to be careful with balloons around heat sources.",
