                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Helpful knowledge: The brightness of a light source is determined by its distance from the observer and its luminosity (intrinsic brightness).\n\nSub-questions:\n1. What is the distance between the observer and the light source?\n2. What is the luminosity of the light source?\n3. How does distance affect the apparent brightness of a light source?\n\nAnswering sub-questions:\n1. The distance between the observer and the light source is an important factor in determining the apparent brightness of the light source. The farther away the light source is, the less bright it will appear to the observer.\n2. The luminosity of the light source is also an important factor in determining its brightness. A more luminous light source will appear brighter than a less luminous one, all other things being equal.\n3. The apparent brightness of a light source decreases with distance according to the inverse square law. This means that if the distance between the observer and the light source is doubled, the apparent brightness of the light source will decrease by a factor of four.\n\nAnswer: Based on the sub-questions, the instance in which light from further away may appear to be less bright than other, closer sources is option B) the moon is brighter than stars. This is because the moon is much closer to Earth than most stars, so even though some stars may be more luminous than the moon, they appear less bright because of their greater distance from Earth.",
