                        "cot_trigger": null,
                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Professor, can you explain what happens when we bang on a drum?\n\nProfessor: Sure, what specifically do you want to know?\n\nStudent: Well, I'm not sure what option to choose. Can you explain each one?\n\nProfessor: Of course. Option A, \"music to be loud,\" is not entirely accurate. While banging on a drum can create noise, it doesn't necessarily create music. Option B, \"music to be appealing,\" is subjective and not a scientific explanation. Option D, \"concerts to sell out,\" is not directly related to the act of banging on a drum. \n\nStudent: Okay, so what about option C, \"reverberations to strike the eardrum\"?\n\nProfessor: That is the correct answer. When a drum is struck, it creates sound waves that travel through the air and eventually reach our ears. These sound waves cause the eardrum to vibrate, which sends signals to our brain that we interpret as sound. The reverberations from the drum are what cause the eardrum to vibrate. \n\nStudent: I see. So, the sound waves are what create the noise, and the reverberations are what make it possible for us to hear it?\n\nProfessor: Exactly. The sound waves are the physical phenomenon that travel through the air, and the reverberations are what allow those sound waves to be detected by our ears. \n\nStudent: Thank you, Professor. That makes a lot of sense now. \n\nProfessor: You're welcome. Always happy to help clarify things.",
                        "answers": [
