                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "Student: Can you explain how phloem moves things around a plant?\n\nProfessor: Sure. Phloem is a type of tissue in plants that transports nutrients and sugars from the leaves to other parts of the plant.\n\nStudent: How does it do that?\n\nProfessor: Phloem uses a process called translocation, which involves the movement of sugars and other nutrients through the plant's vascular system.\n\nStudent: Is it similar to how blood moves in a body?\n\nProfessor: Yes, it is. Just like how blood vessels transport nutrients and oxygen to different parts of the body, phloem transports nutrients and sugars to different parts of the plant.\n\nStudent: What about the other options? Why aren't they correct?\n\nProfessor: Leaves moving in the wind and cars moving on a street are not related to the movement of nutrients in a plant. Water moving in a system is related to the movement of nutrients in a plant, but it is not the best analogy because water does not actively transport nutrients like phloem does.",
