                        "cot_trigger_template": "{instruction}\n\n{question}\n{answer_choices}\n\n{cot_trigger}",
                        "prompt_text": "",
                        "cot": "\n\nFirst, we need to understand what hypothermia is. Hypothermia is a condition where the body temperature drops below normal levels, which can be life-threatening for dogs. In January in Alaska, the temperature can drop to -30\u00b0F, so it's essential to take precautions to keep your dog warm.\n\nOption A: Lots of meat - While a high-protein diet is essential for dogs, it won't necessarily prevent hypothermia. Dogs need insulation to keep them warm, and meat alone won't provide that.\n\nOption B: Lots of love - While love is essential for a dog's well-being, it won't keep them warm in freezing temperatures.\n\nOption C: Vitamin supplements - While vitamins are essential for a dog's health, they won't prevent hypothermia.\n\nOption D: An insulated room - This is the correct answer. An insulated room will provide your dog with a warm and safe place to stay during the cold winter months. You can also provide your dog with a warm bed, blankets, and clothing to help keep them warm when they go outside.\n\nIn conclusion, option D, an insulated room, would help ensure that your dog remains free from hypothermia in January in Alaska.",
